{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DGMYKU9oNBdHUwKnKDh8HM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Area 07: Agent loop, token ledger, deadlines and wrap-up\n2\t\n3\t## 1. Purpose\n4\t\n5\tEvery model interaction in a review goes through one conversation engine:\n6\tthe finder's exploration conversation, each verifier conversation, the\n7\t`doctor` probe, and every single-shot structured completion. This area\n8\tspecifies that engine, independent of any wire protocol:\n9\t\n10\t- the **agent loop**: a multi-turn tool conversation that ends when the\n11\t  model calls the role's **final tool** (for example `submit_findings`)\n12\t  with arguments that validate, and that returns those arguments as the\n13\t  conversation's result;\n14\t- the **structured completion**: the same loop with only a final tool and\n15\t  a one-turn budget;\n16\t- the **wrap-up turn**: the reserved last call in which exploration is\n17\t  over and the model is told to submit what it has, reached by turn\n18\t  exhaustion, the run's **work deadline**, or the **token ledger**;\n19\t- the **token ledger**: one per-run admission controller shared by the\n20\t  finder and verifier clients that reserves each call's worst-case token\n21\t  footprint before it is sent and always keeps enough headroom for the\n22\t  wrap-up submission;\n23\t- the error vocabulary callers use to tell a graceful cutoff (partial\n24\t  results) from an endpoint or credential failure.\n25\t\n26\tGoverning invariant: **no stop path may discard accumulated work**. The\n27\tbudget and the deadline never cancel the wrap-up call; results the model\n28\trecords early (record tools) always run, even after the cutoff.\n29\t\n30\tOut of scope here (owned by the LLM protocols area): HTTP requests, the\n31\tthree wire protocols, SSE parsing, raw response capture into run records,\n32\tsession headers, ambient environment handling, and classifying a provider\n33\terror as retryable. This file assumes the adapter interface in section 3.\n34\t\n35\t## 2. Behavior contract\n36\t\n37\t### 2.1 Terms\n38\t\n39\t- **Conversation**: one call to `converse` or `completeStructured`. It has\n40\t  a system prompt, one initial user message, a tool list, and a final tool\n41\t  name.\n42\t- **Turn**: one loop iteration that sends one request to the model and\n43\t  processes one complete response. `turn` is 1-based. A **step retry**\n44\t  (2.11) and an **in-step switch to wrap-up** (2.8) resend within the same\n45\t  turn; they do not increment `turn`.\n46\t- **Final tool**: the tool whose arguments end the conversation. It has\n47\t  no `run` function; its arguments are validated and returned.\n48\t- **Record tool**: a tool with `recordsResult: true`. It saves part of\n49\t  the result (the finder's `report_finding`) instead of exploring. It\n50\t  runs on the wrap-up turn and after the work deadline.\n51\t- **Exploration tool**: any other tool with a `run` function.\n52\t- **Nudge tool**: the synthetic tool `__roast_continue` that Roast always\n53\t  offers and that Roast itself \"calls\" to keep the loop going after a\n54\t  free-form response (2.6).\n55\t- **Final phase** (`finalTurn` flag): set once the loop is in wrap-up.\n56\t  It never resets within a conversation.\n57\t- **Cutoff**: the reason the final phase was forced by an external limit:\n58\t  `work-deadline` or `budget-exhausted`. At most one cutoff is recorded;\n59\t  the first one wins. Reaching `maxTurns` enters the final phase without\n60\t  a cutoff.\n61\t- **Submission attempt**: a final-phase response that is not record-only\n62\t  (2.7). Counted per complete response, including responses later\n63\t  rejected by required-field validation.\n64\t- `maxAttempts`: the client's `maxAttempts` setting if > 0, else **4**.\n65\t  It bounds three things: step retries (`maxAttempts - 1` retries per\n66\t  request), final-phase submission attempts, and record-only rounds.\n67\t\n68\t### 2.2 Inputs\n69\t\n70\t`ConverseOptions` (all optional unless stated):\n71\t\n72\t| field | meaning | default |\n73\t| --- | --- | --- |\n74\t| `tools` | exploration, record, and final tools, in order (required) | |\n75\t| `finalTool` | name of the final tool; must be in `tools` (required) | |\n76\t| `maxTurns` | turn at which the final phase starts | `<= 0` -> 8 |\n77\t| `onTurn` | progress callback (2.10) | none |\n78\t| `inputByteCeiling` | bytes that trigger the input warning | `<= 0` -> 3e6 |\n79\t| `workDeadline` | absolute time (ms epoch) | none |\n80\t| `wrapUpWindow` | ms bound for wrap-up call, record tools | `<= 0` -> 90000 |\n81\t| `onWorkDeadline` | called once when the work deadline latches | none |\n82\t| `onBudgetExhausted` | called once when the ledger forces wrap-up | none |\n83\t\n84\tClient settings used by the loop (resolved by the config and CLI\n85\tareas): from the role's inference profile, `model` (request model name,\n86\talso used in error messages), `maxOutputTokens` (`max_output_tokens`;\n87\tsent on every request and used by the ledger), and `protocol`; from the\n88\trun's `limits` (flag, then config, then default), `maxAttempts`\n89\t(`max_attempts`) and `requestTimeout` (`request_timeout`, ms per\n90\trequest, 0 = none); and the run's shared `ledger` sized by\n91\t`--max-tokens-total` / `limits.max_total_tokens` (CLI default\n92\t3,000,000; an explicit 0 means no ledger). The CLI defaults\n93\t`max_attempts` to 4 and `request_timeout` to 10 minutes; the loop's own\n94\tfallback for `maxAttempts <= 0` is also 4.\n95\t\n96\tIf `finalTool` names no tool in `tools`, the call fails immediately,\n97\tbefore any request, with the plain error\n98\t`final tool \"<name>\" is not configured`.\n99\t\n100\t### 2.3 Tool set sent to the model\n101\t\n102\t- Every request in a conversation offers the **same** tool set: the\n103\t  caller's tools in caller order, then the nudge tool last. The set\n104\t  never narrows in the final phase (signed reasoning on Anthropic is\n105\t  bound to the tool set; see 2.12). The final phase is enforced through\n106\t  tool results instead.\n107\t- Each caller tool is sent with its **complete** JSON Schema exactly as\n108\t  supplied (including top-level keywords such as\n109\t  `additionalProperties: false`), deep-copied per request so an adapter\n110\t  cannot mutate the caller's copy. `required` lists stay arrays of\n111\t  strings.\n112\t- Nudge tool definition:\n113\t  - name `__roast_continue`\n114\t  - description `Continue the Roast tool loop after a free-form response.`\n115\t  - schema `{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"message\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\n116\t    \"required\":[\"message\"]}`\n117\t- Tool choice is always **automatic**. The loop never forces a tool\n118\t  choice: thinking-mode providers (DeepSeek, Claude with thinking) reject\n119\t  a forced choice. Finalization relies on the notice, validation, and\n120\t  bounded reminders.\n121\t- `completeStructured(system, user, schemaName, finalSpec)` is a\n122\t  conversation with exactly one tool: name `schemaName`, description\n123\t  `Return the requested structured result.`, schema = the result's JSON\n124\t  Schema (no `$schema`, no `$ref`/definitions, objects closed with\n125\t  `additionalProperties: false`), plus the nudge tool. It runs with\n126\t  `maxTurns: 1`, no deadline, and starts in the final phase (2.5).\n127\t\n128\t### 2.4 Transcript\n129\t\n130\tThe transcript is append-only. Order of a request's messages: system\n131\tprompt, initial user message, then for each turn the assistant message,\n132\tits tool-results message, and any user messages Roast injected, each at\n133\tthe position where it was first sent.\n134\t\n135\t- The assistant message records exactly what the model produced, in\n136\t  arrival order: text, reasoning blocks (with any signature or opaque\n137\t  provider payload), and tool calls. Roast never edits, drops, or\n138\t  reorders model output. Synthetic nudge calls (2.6) are appended\n139\t  **after** the model's content.\n140\t- The tool-results message has one result per dispatched call, in the\n141\t  order the model called the tools (not completion order). A result is\n142\t  either a success text or an error text (`isError: true`).\n143\t- An injected user message (input warning, wrap-up notice) is appended\n144\t  once, when first needed, and keeps its position for the rest of the\n145\t  conversation; every later request, retry, and reminder resends it.\n146\t\n147\t### 2.5 Turn preparation (before each turn's request)\n148\t\n149\tIn this order:\n150\t\n151\t1. `turn += 1`.\n152\t2. **Input warning**: if `turn > 1`, the warning was not yet sent, and the\n153\t   transcript's byte size (2.13) exceeds `inputByteCeiling`, append once\n154\t   the user message\n155\t   `INPUT BUDGET EXHAUSTED. Do not request more files. Call \\`<final>\\`\n156\t   now with what you have \\u2014 partial results beat none.`\n157\t   (one line; `\\u2014` is an em dash in the actual string). This does\n158\t   **not** enter the final phase.\n159\t3. **Work deadline**: if the deadline check (2.8) reports fired, force the\n160\t   final phase with cutoff `work-deadline`.\n161\t4. **Turn budget**: if `turn >= normalizedMaxTurns`, force the final\n162\t   phase (no cutoff). So `maxTurns: 4` gives three exploration turns and\n163\t   makes turn 4 the wrap-up turn; `maxTurns: 1` starts in wrap-up.\n164\t5. **Wrap-up notice**: if in the final phase and no notice was sent yet,\n165\t   append the notice (2.9) as a user message and mark it sent. For a\n166\t   structured completion the notice is always\n167\t   ``Return the requested structured result via the `<final>` tool call.\n168\t   Do not answer in prose.``\n169\t\n170\t### 2.6 Processing one complete response\n171\t\n172\tA response is complete when the adapter delivered a finish event. Before\n173\tanything is dispatched:\n174\t\n175\t1. Raw response capture (protocols area) must have succeeded; a capture\n176\t   failure fails the turn with a non-retryable error (2.11).\n177\t2. If the turn's signal was aborted (caller cancel, request timeout, work\n178\t   deadline), the turn fails with that abort; buffered tool calls are\n179\t   **never** dispatched.\n180\t3. No finish event (stream ended early) -> incomplete-stream error, which\n181\t   is retryable (2.11). Partial tool calls are never dispatched.\n182\t\n183\tThen, by finish reason category (`stop`, `length`, `content-filter`,\n184\t`tool-calls`, `error`, `other`, `unknown`):\n185\t\n186\t- **Abnormal finish** (anything but `stop` / `tool-calls`): nothing is\n187\t  dispatched, not even a final call; the conversation ends. Set\n188\t  `stopCause` = `the response ended with finish reason \"<category>\"`,\n189\t  then append ` (the provider sent \"<raw>\")` when the adapter supplied a\n190\t  raw provider reason that differs from the category, then, for `length`\n191\t  only, append `; raise max_output_tokens if the output was cut off`.\n192\t  A length finish is not retried.\n193\t- **`stop` or `tool-calls`**: run **review** below. Its outcome says which\n194\t  calls to dispatch (if any), which synthetic nudge calls to append, and\n195\t  whether the conversation continues.\n196\t\n197\t**Review** (`stopCause` is cleared first). Let `calls` be the model's\n198\ttool calls in order, `final` the **last** call naming the final tool,\n199\t`records` the number of record-tool calls. Any call naming a tool that is\n200\tneither a caller tool nor the nudge tool is reported to `onTurn`\n201\timmediately (2.10).\n202\t\n203\tA. Final phase, no final call, `records > 0`, and every call is a record\n204\t   call (**record-only round**): increment `recordRounds`. Submission\n205\t   attempts are **not** incremented. If finalization may not continue\n206\t   (2.8: a cutoff exists, after latching the deadline) -> set `halt` with\n207\t   stopCause ``the model recorded results but did not call `<final>`\n208\t   before the cutoff``. Else if `recordRounds > maxAttempts` -> set `halt`\n209\t   with stopCause ``the model kept recording results without calling\n210\t   `<final>` (<n> recording rounds)``. Dispatch all calls.\n211\t\n212\tB. Otherwise, if in the final phase, `submissionAttempts += 1`.\n213\t\n214\tC. A final call is present:\n215\t   - Every exploration call in this response gets the error result\n216\t     ``Not executed: this response also called `<final>`, which ends the\n217\t     run.`` and is not run. Record calls still run.\n218\t   - **Type validation** of the final arguments (2.7). If it fails, the\n219\t     loop is in the final phase, and another submission may be made\n220\t     (`canRetrySubmission`: finalization may continue and\n221\t     `submissionAttempts < maxAttempts`): this is a provisional\n222\t     rejection. Clear any earlier `finalErr`. Every final call in the\n223\t     response gets the error result ``your previous response was not\n224\t     valid JSON matching the schema: <err>. Call `<final>` again with\n225\t     valid arguments.`` Set stopCause\n226\t     ``the last `<final>` call did not match the schema``.\n227\t     Dispatch and continue.\n228\t   - Otherwise set `finalErr` to the validation error (or clear it on\n229\t     success). A failed validation here is terminal\n230\t     (`validationExhausted`): the final call gets the error result\n231\t     `<err>` and the conversation stops. This includes an invalid final\n232\t     call during exploration: it ends the run immediately, without a\n233\t     reminder.\n234\t   - On successful type validation, the final call becomes the accepted\n235\t     submission. Set stopCause ``the last `<final>` call did not pass\n236\t     tool-call validation`` (used only if required-field validation, 2.7,\n237\t     rejects it). Dispatch in order:\n238\t     - another final call that is not the accepted one (different call ID\n239\t       or different arguments text) gets the error result\n240\t       ``Not accepted: call `<final>` once per response.``;\n241\t     - the accepted call, if it passes required-field validation,\n242\t       completes the conversation: the result is the parsed arguments,\n243\t       its tool result text is the raw arguments string, and the loop\n244\t       stops after this response's dispatch;\n245\t     - a final call dispatched after the conversation already completed\n246\t       (for example a duplicate of the accepted call) gets\n247\t       `Already submitted.` and stops the turn.\n248\t\n249\tD. Final phase, no final call (prose, exploration calls, or unknown\n250\t   tools, possibly mixed with record calls):\n251\t   - Exploration calls get the error result\n252\t     `Not executed: exploration is finished.`; record calls still run.\n253\t   - If `canRetrySubmission`: append a nudge call with ID\n254\t     `roast-final-<submissionAttempts>` and message\n255\t     ``Exploration is finished. Return the result via the `<final>` tool\n256\t     call, not prose or another tool.``; dispatch and continue.\n257\t   - Else set stopCause ``the model answered without calling\n258\t     `<final>` `` (no calls) or ``the model called other tools instead of\n259\t     `<final>` `` (some calls), then append\n260\t     ` (<n> submission attempt)` / ` (<n> submission attempts)` (singular\n261\t     exactly when n = 1). If `records > 0`, set `halt` and dispatch (the\n262\t     record calls run; everything else is blocked as above). Otherwise\n263\t     dispatch nothing; the conversation ends.\n264\t\n265\tE. Exploration phase, `calls` non-empty: dispatch all calls normally.\n266\t\n267\tF. Exploration phase, no calls (prose only): append a nudge call with ID\n268\t   `roast-nudge-<turn>` and message ``Results must be delivered via the\n269\t   `<final>` tool call. If you have enough context call it now; otherwise\n270\t   call an exploration tool.``; dispatch and continue.\n271\t\n272\tThe nudge tool's result is its `message` argument as success text. A\n273\tnudge counts as a dispatched call, so the turn continues.\n274\t\n275\t### 2.7 Validating and dispatching tool calls\n276\t\n277\tDispatch is **sequential**, in the model's call order: one tool finishes\n278\t(or is abandoned, 2.8) before the next starts. Before a call is run it\n279\tpasses **required-field validation** (applies to every tool, including\n280\tthe final tool and the nudge tool):\n281\t\n282\t- unknown tool -> error result\n283\t  `tool not found: <name>. Available tools: <n1>, <n2>, ...` (the caller\n284\t  tools then the nudge tool, comma-space separated);\n285\t- arguments are not a JSON object -> error result\n286\t  `invalid JSON input: <parse error>`;\n287\t- a top-level `required` property of the tool's schema is absent ->\n288\t  error result `missing required parameter: <name>` (first missing name\n289\t  in `required` order).\n290\t\n291\tNo repair of malformed arguments is ever attempted (Go disabled\n292\tFantasy's JSON repair). A call that fails this validation is not run.\n293\tFor the final tool this means no completion; the conversation continues\n294\tif the response continues (2.10), and the stop conditions bound it. This\n295\tvalidation does not consume anything beyond the submission attempt\n296\talready counted in review B.\n297\t\n298\t**Type validation** of final arguments (review C) mirrors decoding the\n299\tJSON into the caller's typed result:\n300\t\n301\t- the text must be valid JSON (else the error is `invalid JSON`);\n302\t- every property that is present must have a compatible type (a string\n303\t  where a string is expected, an integer where an integer is expected,\n304\t  and so on, recursively);\n305\t- property names match exactly (case-sensitive; deliberate change 7), so\n306\t  `Name` never satisfies `name`; a JSON `null` for any property counts as\n307\t  absent (Go parity), not as a type error;\n308\t- absent properties are allowed here (required-field validation catches\n309\t  top-level ones later); unknown properties are ignored;\n310\t- **integers are preserved exactly**: an integer beyond\n311\t  `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (for example `9007199254740993`) must reach\n312\t  the caller unchanged, never rounded. Parse the arguments with a reviver\n313\t  that reads the literal source text and yields a `bigint` for unsafe\n314\t  integers; the result schema accepts `bigint` for 64-bit integer fields.\n315\t\n316\tRunning an exploration or record call:\n317\t\n318\t- A blocked call (review C/D) returns its block message as an error\n319\t  result and is not reported to `onTurn`.\n320\t- A tool with no `run` gets the error result `{\"error\":\"tool has no\n321\t  implementation\"}` (as success text, see below).\n322\t- `run(args, signal)` receives the decoded argument object. If it throws\n323\t  or rejects, or its wait is cut short (2.8), the result value is\n324\t  `{\"error\": \"<message>\"}`.\n325\t- The result value is serialized as JSON and sent as **success** text\n326\t  (tool errors are data for the model, not error results). If\n327\t  serialization fails, send `{\"error\": \"<serialization error>\"}`. An\n328\t  `undefined` / `null` result serializes as `null`.\n329\t- Serialization uses the llm area's one JSON encoder (spec.md\n330\t  cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\"): object keys are sorted, matching Go's\n331\t  map ordering so prompts stay stable, and there is no HTML escaping of\n332\t  `<`, `>`, `&`, U+2028 or U+2029. Parity with Go is on parsed JSON, not\n333\t  bytes.\n334\t\n335\t### 2.8 Work deadline and wrap-up window\n336\t\n337\tThe work deadline is a **latched clock check**, never a timeout around\n338\tthe whole conversation (that would cancel the wrap-up turn along with the\n339\twork).\n340\t\n341\t- `check()`: if already fired, return true. If there is no deadline or\n342\t  `now < deadline`, return false. Otherwise latch `fired = true`, call\n343\t  `onWorkDeadline` exactly once, and return true.\n344\t- Request signal for a model call: the caller's signal, combined with\n345\t  - before the latch, a timer that fires at the deadline (so an in-flight\n346\t    exploration call cannot run past it);\n347\t  - after the latch, a fresh `wrapUpWindow` timeout (so a stuck wrap-up\n348\t    cannot hang the run);\n349\t  - and, if `requestTimeout > 0`, a per-request timeout.\n350\t- **In-flight deadline**: if an exploration call (not final phase) fails\n351\t  for any reason, `check()` is true afterwards, and the caller's signal is\n352\t  not aborted (and the failure was not a capture failure), the loop forces\n353\t  the final phase with cutoff `work-deadline` and immediately sends the\n354\t  wrap-up call **within the same turn** (in-step switch, 2.9). The\n355\t  failed call is not retried.\n356\t- After every model call, `check()` runs (latching a deadline that passed\n357\t  during the call).\n358\t- **Exploration tool wait**: before running an exploration call, if\n359\t  `check()` is true, the call is not started and its result is\n360\t  `{\"error\":\"llm: work deadline reached\"}` (later calls of a multi-tool\n361\t  response short-circuit this way). Otherwise the tool runs with a signal\n362\t  bounded by the deadline (or unbounded with no deadline). The loop races\n363\t  the tool's promise against that signal: on abort it stops waiting,\n364\t  records the abort reason as the error, and abandons the promise (its\n365\t  outcome is ignored). Tools that spawn a process kill it on abort\n366\t  (spec.md \"Process lifetime\"), so an abandoned tool does not keep Node\n367\t  alive; the CLI also calls `process.exit` after flushing output.\n368\t  `check()` runs after either outcome.\n369\t- **Record tool wait**: always bounded by a fresh `wrapUpWindow` timeout\n370\t  from the caller's signal, before or after the deadline, so work\n371\t  reported at the cutoff is kept rather than interrupted.\n372\t- `mayContinueFinalization()`: run `check()`; if fired, force the final\n373\t  phase with cutoff `work-deadline`; return `cutoff === none`. After any\n374\t  cutoff the loop never sends a reminder and never opens a second wrap-up\n375\t  window: the wrap-up turn gets exactly one response.\n376\t- `canRetrySubmission()` = `mayContinueFinalization() &&\n377\t  submissionAttempts < maxAttempts`.\n378\t\n379\t### 2.9 Forced wrap-up: notice text and in-step switch\n380\t\n381\tNotice text, chosen when it is first sent (`<final>` is the final tool):\n382\t\n383\t- cutoff `budget-exhausted`:\n384\t  ``TOKEN BUDGET EXHAUSTED \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with everything you\n385\t  have; partial results beat none.``\n386\t- cutoff `work-deadline` and the deadline latched:\n387\t  ``WORK DEADLINE REACHED \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with everything you\n388\t  have; partial results beat none.``\n389\t- otherwise (turn budget, or a `work-deadline` cutoff whose latch did not\n390\t  happen): ``Exploration budget exhausted \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with\n391\t  everything you have; partial results beat none.``\n392\t- structured completion: the structured notice in 2.5 replaces all of the\n393\t  above.\n394\t\n395\tEach is one line in the actual string; `\\u2014` is an em dash.\n396\t\n397\t**In-step switch** (budget refusal of an exploration call, 2.13, or an\n398\tin-flight deadline, 2.8): set the final phase, append the notice if none\n399\twas sent yet, rebuild the request from the transcript (same tool set),\n400\tand send it as a final-phase call in the same turn. That response is\n401\tprocessed like any final-phase response (review B-D). The refused or\n402\tfailed exploration request leaves no trace in the transcript.\n403\t\n404\t### 2.10 Stop conditions, callbacks, and continuation\n405\t\n406\tAfter each turn's dispatch, evaluate in order, short-circuiting:\n407\t\n408\t1. the conversation completed (accepted final call);\n409\t2. `validationExhausted`;\n410\t3. `halt`;\n411\t4. final phase and `submissionAttempts >= maxAttempts`;\n412\t5. **deadline during finalization**: final phase and `check()` is true ->\n413\t   force cutoff `work-deadline` (if no cutoff yet) and, if `stopCause` is\n414\t   empty, set it to ``the work deadline passed before `<final>` was\n415\t   called``.\n416\t\n417\tStop if any is true. Otherwise continue only if this turn dispatched at\n418\tleast one call (including nudges) and no result requested a stop (an\n419\taccepted final call or `Already submitted.`). A response that dispatches\n420\tnothing ends the conversation. After dispatch, if the caller's signal is\n421\taborted, stop with the abort (2.14) instead of continuing.\n422\t\n423\t`maxTurns` does not stop the loop by itself. The final phase is bounded\n424\tby submission attempts, record rounds, cutoffs, and the deadline.\n425\t\n426\tCallbacks:\n427\t\n428\t- `onTurn({turn, tool, args})` is called after each **dispatched**\n429\t  exploration or record call, whether it succeeded, failed, or was\n430\t  refused by the deadline. It is also called, during review, for each\n431\t  call to an unknown tool (nothing else would report it). It is never\n432\t  called for the final tool, the nudge tool, blocked calls, or calls that\n433\t  failed required-field validation. `args` is the decoded argument\n434\t  object, or `{\"error\": \"args parse error: <message>\"}` if the arguments\n435\t  do not parse as a JSON object. `turn` is the current turn number.\n436\t- `onWorkDeadline()`: exactly once, at the first latch (2.8). It fires\n437\t  even if the wrap-up submission then succeeds, so callers can mark\n438\t  coverage as partial.\n439\t- `onBudgetExhausted()`: at most once per conversation, when the ledger\n440\t  refuses an exploration call, or refuses a final-phase call while no\n441\t  cutoff is recorded yet. Fires even if the wrap-up then succeeds.\n442\t\n443\t### 2.11 Step retries\n444\t\n445\tEach turn's request (and each in-step wrap-up request) is retried when it\n446\tfails with a retryable error, up to `maxAttempts - 1` retries:\n447\t\n448\t- Retryable (classified by the adapter): HTTP 408, 409, 429, any 5xx; an\n449\t  error flagged transient; a response header `x-should-retry` (any case)\n450\t  parsing as true; an unexpectedly ended stream (no finish event);\n451\t  network/transport errors (DNS, connection refused/reset, TCP timeout,\n452\t  HTTP/2 stream errors).\n453\t- Never retried: aborts (caller cancel, request timeout, deadline\n454\t  signal), capture failures, ledger refusals, any error after a cutoff\n455\t  in the final phase (2.14 wraps it into a cutoff error), and every\n456\t  other error.\n457\t- Delay: initial 5,000 ms, doubled after each retry. A `retry-after-ms`\n458\t  header (milliseconds, float) or else `retry-after` (seconds, float, or\n459\t  an HTTP date) replaces the delay when the parsed value is > 0 and\n460\t  (< 60 s or < the backoff delay). The wait aborts with the caller's\n461\t  signal.\n462\t- Each retry re-runs ledger admission (the previous reservation was\n463\t  released with zero usage) and resends the same transcript; any\n464\t  provisional state from the failed attempt is discarded.\n465\t- When retries are exhausted the error reports the last attempt's error\n466\t  (Go prefixed `retry error: `).\n467\t- `maxAttempts: 1` means no retries.\n468\t\n469\t### 2.12 Signed reasoning and conversation identity\n470\t\n471\t- Signed reasoning (Claude 5.1 and later) is bound to the system prompt,\n472\t  the tool set, and every earlier message. Hence: the tool set is\n473\t  constant (2.3), model output is replayed verbatim (2.4), corrections\n474\t  travel as tool results rather than edits, and injected messages never\n475\t  move.\n476\t- Each conversation gets a session ID `roast-<26 random characters from\n477\t  A-Z2-7>` (128 bits, base32), generated once per `converse` /\n478\t  `completeStructured` call and passed to the adapter for every request,\n479\t  retry, reminder, and correction of that conversation. Distinct\n480\t  conversations get distinct IDs. (The header itself is the protocols\n481\t  area's.)\n482\t\n483\t### 2.13 Token ledger\n484\t\n485\tOne ledger per run, shared by the finder and verifier clients (concurrent\n486\tverifier conversations reserve against the same ledger). Units are\n487\t\"input-equivalent tokens\". Constants:\n488\t\n489\t- `EST_BYTES_PER_TOKEN = 3` (conservative tokenization: code-heavy\n490\t  prompts run about 3 bytes/token, so this over-estimates, the safe\n491\t  direction for admission);\n492\t- `DEFAULT_FINAL_ALLOWANCE = 100_000`;\n493\t- `CACHED_TOKEN_WEIGHT = 0.1` (cache reads bill about 0.1x).\n494\t\n495\tConstruction: `newLedger(limit)` returns **no ledger** (unlimited) when\n496\t`limit <= 0`. Otherwise `finalAllowance = min(100_000, limit / 2)`\n497\t(floating point, not rounded). All arithmetic is floating point.\n498\tState: `committed`, `outstanding` (both start at 0), `exhausted` (false).\n499\t\n500\t`reserve(reqBytes, maxOutput, final)`:\n501\t\n502\t```\n503\test = reqBytes / 3 + maxOutput\n504\theadroom = limit\n505\tif not final:\n506\t    headroom -= max(finalAllowance, est + maxOutput)\n507\tif committed + outstanding + est > headroom:\n508\t    exhausted = true\n509\t    refuse\n510\toutstanding += est\n511\treturn release\n512\t```\n513\t\n514\tThe non-final hold-back covers this request's own footprint plus a\n515\tworst-case response, because the wrap-up call replays the same transcript\n516\tgrown by this call's output; a smaller hold-back could admit exploration\n517\tthe final flush can no longer afford. A final reservation may use the\n518\twhole limit.\n519\t\n520\t`release(usage)` must be called exactly once per successful reservation;\n521\textra calls are no-ops. It does `outstanding -= est` and\n522\t`committed += weighted(usage)` where\n523\t`weighted = max(uncached,0) + 0.1 * max(cached,0) + max(cacheWrite,0)\n524\t+ max(output,0)`. Each field is floored independently; cached may exceed\n525\tuncached legitimately and is never clamped against it.\n526\t\n527\t`exhausted()` is true once any reservation was refused (final or not);\n528\tthe verify stage reads it to stop launching verifications.\n529\t`committed()` returns `{spent: trunc(committed), limit: trunc(limit)}`\n530\t(`{0, 0}` with no ledger), for the run record's budget provenance.\n531\t\n532\tWith no ledger every reservation is admitted and release is a no-op.\n533\t\n534\t**Admission in the loop.** Every model request is reserved immediately\n535\tbefore it is sent:\n536\t\n537\t- `reqBytes` = the adapter-reported byte size of the serialized request\n538\t  body + 2048. (Go measured its internal transcript JSON plus the tool\n539\t  definitions JSON plus 2048.) Admission near the limit can therefore\n540\t  differ from Go (accepted, spec.md \"Resolved open questions\").\n541\t- `maxOutput` = the client's `maxOutputTokens`.\n542\t- `final` = the loop is in the final phase.\n543\t- Release with the normalized usage (2.15) when the response delivered a\n544\t  finish event, and with zero usage in every other outcome (error, abort,\n545\t  capture failure, early stop). Release happens on every path, exactly\n546\t  once.\n547\t\n548\tOn refusal:\n549\t\n550\t- exploration request: force the final phase with cutoff\n551\t  `budget-exhausted`, fire `onBudgetExhausted`, and switch in-step to\n552\t  the wrap-up call (2.9). The refused request is not sent.\n553\t- final-phase request: if no cutoff is recorded yet, record\n554\t  `budget-exhausted` and fire `onBudgetExhausted`. The turn fails with\n555\t  the cutoff error ``...; no headroom for the wrap-up call: ...`` (2.14),\n556\t  not retried.\n557\t\n558\tSo a final-phase reminder is still subject to admission: after a\n559\tresponse whose reported usage spent the budget, the next reminder is\n560\trefused and the conversation ends with the budget cutoff.\n561\t\n562\t**Transcript bytes** (for the input warning, 2.5): the UTF-8 byte length\n563\tof the JSON serialization of the transcript's messages, system prompt\n564\tincluded, tool definitions excluded.\n565\t\n566\t### 2.14 Errors and result precedence\n567\t\n568\tSentinels (message texts are the Go texts):\n569\t\n570\t| sentinel | message |\n571\t| --- | --- |\n572\t| `no-final-call` | `llm: tool loop ended without a final tool call` |\n573\t| `work-deadline` | `llm: work deadline reached` |\n574\t| `budget-exhausted` | `llm: run token budget exhausted` |\n575\t| `auth` | `llm: authentication failed` |\n576\t| `endpoint` | `llm: inference endpoint failure` |\n577\t\n578\t`no-final-call` means finalization ran out of bounded attempts without a\n579\tvalid submission: the finder reports incomplete coverage; the verifier\n580\tleaves the candidate unverified (never refuted). The CLI treats\n581\t`budget-exhausted`, `work-deadline`, and `no-final-call` as graceful.\n582\t\n583\t**Cutoff error** `cutoffError(what, cause?)`: matches the cutoff sentinel\n584\t(or `no-final-call` if no cutoff) **and** `no-final-call`. Message:\n585\t`<cutoff sentinel message>; <what>: <no-final-call message>` followed by\n586\t`: <cause message>` when there is a cause. The cause contributes text\n587\tonly; it is not matchable (a `budget-exhausted` cause inside a\n588\t`work-deadline` cutoff does not make the error match\n589\t`budget-exhausted`). The provider's own error text appears after the\n590\tgeneric sentinel, and the `no-final-call` text appears exactly once.\n591\t\n592\t**Provider error normalization** `normalize(err)`:\n593\t\n594\t1. caller's signal aborted -> the caller's abort reason, unwrapped;\n595\t2. err is an abort/timeout (for example `requestTimeout`) -> `endpoint`\n596\t   error `llm: inference endpoint failure: <model>: <err>`, keeping err\n597\t   as the cause (so a timeout stays detectable);\n598\t3. err already matches `auth`, `endpoint`, or `budget-exhausted` -> as is;\n599\t4. HTTP status 401 or 403 -> `auth` error\n600\t   `llm: authentication failed: <err>; check the configured API key`;\n601\t5. anything else -> `endpoint` error as in 2.\n602\t\n603\t**Final-phase request failure after a cutoff** (the request fails and a\n604\tcutoff is recorded): the turn fails with\n605\t`cutoffError(\"wrap-up call failed\", normalize(err))`, which is never\n606\tretried. A capture failure is reported unwrapped instead.\n607\t\n608\t**Result precedence** when the loop ends:\n609\t\n610\t1. accepted final call -> return the parsed result.\n611\t2. `finalErr` set -> error\n612\t   `<model>: <final> arguments did not satisfy the schema (<err>); if\n613\t   truncated, raise max_output_tokens`; if a cutoff is recorded, wrap it\n614\t   as `cutoffError(\"wrap-up submission was invalid\", that)`.\n615\t   (`finalErr` is cleared on each provisional rejection, so an earlier\n616\t   schema failure never masks a later provider failure or a later\n617\t   missing submission.)\n618\t3. the loop failed with an error:\n619\t   - already a cutoff error (matches `no-final-call`) -> as is;\n620\t   - a cutoff is recorded -> `cutoffError(\"wrap-up turn failed\", err)`;\n621\t   - otherwise `normalize(err)`.\n622\t4. no error: let `cause = \"<model>: <stopCause>\"` if stopCause is set.\n623\t   - cutoff recorded -> `cutoffError(\"wrap-up turn produced no\n624\t     submission\", cause)`;\n625\t   - else `no-final-call` error `<no-final-call message>: <cause>`, or\n626\t     the bare `no-final-call` error without a cause.\n627\t\n628\tCaller cancellation therefore surfaces as the raw abort reason (unless a\n629\tcutoff was already recorded, in which case it is the cause text of a\n630\tcutoff error).\n631\t\n632\t### 2.15 Usage accounting\n633\t\n634\tAdapters report per-response counts: `input`, `cacheRead`, `cacheWrite`,\n635\t`output`. Each is floored at 0. The client normalizes them once per\n636\tfinished response into **disjoint** counts. The per-protocol rules are\n637\towned by the llm-protocols area, which subtracts cached tokens exactly\n638\tonce for `chat` and `responses` (deliberate change 13; Go subtracted\n639\tthem twice); `anthropic` counts are disjoint already. The loop and\n640\tledger only consume the resulting `Usage`, where `uncached_input_tokens`,\n641\t`cached_input_tokens`, and `cache_write_tokens` never overlap.\n642\t\n643\t`Usage = {uncached_input_tokens, cached_input_tokens, cache_write_tokens,\n644\toutput_tokens, calls}` with `calls = 1` per finished response. The client\n645\taccumulates it across all its conversations (`client.usage()`), and the\n646\tsame per-response value reconciles the ledger. Responses without a finish\n647\tevent add nothing. Example (chat): `prompt_tokens` 8 of which 4 cached,\n648\t`completion_tokens` 3 -> uncached 4, cached 4, write 0, output 3,\n649\tcalls 1. Its ledger weight is `4 + 0.1 * 4 + 0 + 3 = 7.4`.\n650\t\n651\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n652\t\n653\tAll modules live under `src/llm/`. The loop is written as a small\n654\timperative shell (`agent.ts`) around pure decision functions\n655\t(`review.ts`, `ledger.ts` math, `errors.ts`), so most invariants are unit\n656\ttestable with a scripted fake adapter and an injected clock.\n657\t\n658\t### 3.1 Adapter interface (assumed; owned by the LLM protocols area)\n659\t\n660\t```ts\n661\texport type FinishCategory = 'stop'|'length'|'content-filter'|\n662\t    'tool-calls'|'error'|'other'|'unknown'\n663\t\n664\texport interface RawUsage {\n665\t    input:number\n666\t    cacheRead:number\n667\t    cacheWrite:number\n668\t    output:number\n669\t}\n670\t\n671\texport type StreamEvent =\n672\t    | {type:'text', text:string}\n673\t    | {type:'reasoning', part:ReasoningPart}  // opaque, replayed verbatim\n674\t    | {type:'tool-call', id:string, name:string, input:string}\n675\t    | {type:'finish', reason:FinishCategory, rawReason?:string,\n676\t        usage:RawUsage}\n677\t\n678\texport interface ModelRequest {\n679\t    model:string\n680\t    system:string\n681\t    messages:readonly Message[]\n682\t    tools:readonly ToolDefinition[]  // name, description, full schema\n683\t    toolChoice:'auto'\n684\t    maxOutputTokens:number\n685\t    sessionId:string\n686\t}\n687\t\n688\texport interface ProtocolAdapter {\n689\t    readonly protocol:'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\n690\t    // Exact byte size of the serialized request body.\n691\t    requestBytes(req:ModelRequest):number\n692\t    // Yields events in arrival order; throws on transport/HTTP/stream\n693\t    // errors. Capture failures throw CaptureError. Thrown errors expose\n694\t    // `retryable:boolean` and, for HTTP failures, `status:number`.\n695\t    stream(req:ModelRequest, signal:AbortSignal):AsyncIterable<StreamEvent>\n696\t}\n697\t```\n698\t\n699\tThe loop collects a whole response before acting on it; it does not\n700\tforward partial text anywhere.\n701\t\n702\t### 3.2 Transcript (`src/llm/messages.ts`)\n703\t\n704\t```ts\n705\texport type AssistantPart =\n706\t    | {type:'text', text:string}\n707\t    | {type:'reasoning', part:ReasoningPart}\n708\t    | {type:'tool-call', id:string, name:string, input:string}\n709\t\n710\texport interface ToolResult {\n711\t    callId:string\n712\t    name:string\n713\t    text:string\n714\t    isError:boolean\n715\t}\n716\t\n717\texport type Message =\n718\t    | {role:'user', text:string}\n719\t    | {role:'assistant', parts:readonly AssistantPart[]}\n720\t    | {role:'tool', results:readonly ToolResult[]}\n721\t\n722\texport function transcriptBytes(system:string,\n723\t    messages:readonly Message[]):number\n724\t```\n725\t\n726\t### 3.3 Public types (`src/llm/types.ts`)\n727\t\n728\t```ts\n729\texport interface Tool {\n730\t    name:string\n731\t    description:string\n732\t    schema:JsonSchemaObject\n733\t    // Absent for the final tool.\n734\t    run?:(args:Record<string, unknown>, signal:AbortSignal) =>\n735\t        unknown|Promise<unknown>\n736\t    recordsResult?:boolean\n737\t}\n738\t\n739\texport interface TurnInfo {\n740\t    turn:number\n741\t    tool:string\n742\t    args:Record<string, unknown>\n743\t}\n744\t\n745\t// Type validation of the final arguments (2.7). `decode` receives the\n746\t// value parsed with integer preservation and throws an Error whose\n747\t// message is the validation detail.\n748\texport interface FinalSpec<T> {\n749\t    decode(value:unknown):T\n750\t}\n751\t\n752\texport interface ConverseOptions<T> {\n753\t    tools:readonly Tool[]\n754\t    finalTool:string\n755\t    final:FinalSpec<T>\n756\t    maxTurns?:number\n757\t    onTurn?:(info:TurnInfo) => void\n758\t    inputByteCeiling?:number\n759\t    workDeadline?:number      // ms since epoch\n760\t    wrapUpWindow?:number      // ms\n761\t    onWorkDeadline?:() => void\n762\t    onBudgetExhausted?:() => void\n763\t}\n764\t\n765\texport interface Usage {\n766\t    uncached_input_tokens:number\n767\t    cached_input_tokens:number\n768\t    cache_write_tokens:number\n769\t    output_tokens:number\n770\t    calls:number\n771\t}\n772\t\n773\texport interface Client {\n774\t    readonly name:string  // request model name\n775\t    converse<T>(system:string, user:string, opts:ConverseOptions<T>,\n776\t        signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n777\t    completeStructured<T>(system:string, user:string, schemaName:string,\n778\t        result:{schema:JsonSchemaObject, decode:FinalSpec<T>['decode']},\n779\t        signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n780\t    usage():Usage\n781\t}\n782\t```\n783\t\n784\t`Usage` keeps snake_case keys because it is serialized into run records\n785\tas is.\n786\t\n787\t### 3.4 Modules\n788\t\n789\t- `src/llm/errors.ts`\n790\t  ```ts\n791\t  export type Sentinel = 'no-final-call'|'work-deadline'|\n792\t      'budget-exhausted'|'auth'|'endpoint'\n793\t  export interface ErrorDetails {\n794\t      model?:string\n795\t      finishReason?:FinishCategory\n796\t      rawFinishReason?:string\n797\t      submissionAttempts?:number\n798\t      recordRounds?:number\n799\t      stopCause?:StopCauseKind\n800\t  }\n801\t  export class LlmError extends Error {\n802\t      readonly sentinels:ReadonlySet<Sentinel>\n803\t      readonly details:ErrorDetails\n804\t  }\n805\t  export function hasSentinel(err:unknown, s:Sentinel):boolean\n806\t  export function cutoffError(cutoff:Sentinel|null, what:string,\n807\t      cause?:unknown):LlmError\n808\t  export function normalizeProviderError(callerSignal:AbortSignal,\n809\t      model:string, err:unknown):unknown\n810\t  export class CaptureError extends Error {}\n811\t  ```\n812\t  `hasSentinel` walks `sentinels` and the `cause` chain (so an endpoint\n813\t  error wrapping a timeout still exposes the timeout via `cause`).\n814\t  `cutoffError` must not put the cause in the matchable chain (2.14):\n815\t  store it as `details`-only text, not as `cause`. `details` lets tests\n816\t  assert the stop reason, counts, and finish reason without matching\n817\t  message text. This module is shared with the protocols area, which\n818\t  raises `auth` / `endpoint` errors.\n819\t\n820\t- `src/llm/ledger.ts`\n821\t  ```ts\n822\t  export const EST_BYTES_PER_TOKEN = 3\n823\t  export const DEFAULT_FINAL_ALLOWANCE = 100_000\n824\t  export const CACHED_TOKEN_WEIGHT = 0.1\n825\t  export type Release = (usage:Usage) => void\n826\t  export class Ledger {\n827\t      reserve(reqBytes:number, maxOutput:number,\n828\t          final:boolean):Release|null   // null = refused\n829\t      exhausted():boolean\n830\t      committed():{spent:number, limit:number}\n831\t  }\n832\t  export function newLedger(limit:number):Ledger|null\n833\t  export function reserve(ledger:Ledger|null, reqBytes:number,\n834\t      maxOutput:number, final:boolean):Release|null\n835\t  export function weightedTokens(usage:Usage):number\n836\t  ```\n837\t  All methods are synchronous, so concurrent verifier promises cannot\n838\t  interleave inside a reservation. `Release` is idempotent.\n839\t\n840\t- `src/llm/usage.ts`: `normalizeUsage(protocol, raw):Usage` (2.15) and\n841\t  `addUsage(total, u):Usage`.\n842\t\n843\t- `src/llm/deadline.ts`\n844\t  ```ts\n845\t  export class WorkDeadline {\n846\t      constructor(opts:{deadline?:number, window?:number,\n847\t          onFire?:() => void, now?:() => number})\n848\t      get fired():boolean\n849\t      check():boolean\n850\t      // Signal for a model call: caller + deadline timer before the\n851\t      // latch, caller + fresh window after it.\n852\t      callSignal(caller:AbortSignal):AbortSignal\n853\t      runTool(tool:Tool, args:Record<string, unknown>,\n854\t          caller:AbortSignal):Promise<{value:unknown}|{error:Error}>\n855\t  }\n856\t  ```\n857\t  `callSignal` and `runTool` use `AbortSignal.any` with\n858\t  `AbortSignal.timeout` (window, request timeout) or a timer aborting at\n859\t  the absolute deadline. `runTool` races the tool against the signal and\n860\t  never awaits an abandoned tool.\n861\t\n862\t- `src/llm/final-args.ts`\n863\t  - `parsePreservingIntegers(text:string):unknown` -- `JSON.parse` with a\n864\t    reviver using the source-text context argument; an integer literal\n865\t    outside the safe range becomes `bigint`. Throws on invalid JSON.\n866\t  - `checkRequired(schema, input:string):string|null` -- the\n867\t    required-field validation of 2.7; returns the error text or null.\n868\t  - `typeValidate<T>(text, spec:FinalSpec<T>)` ->\n869\t    `{ok:true, value:T}|{ok:false, error:string}` (`invalid JSON` on\n870\t    parse failure).\n871\t\n872\t- `src/llm/tool-json.ts`: `goJsonStringify(value):string` -- JSON with\n873\t  sorted keys and no HTML escaping (2.7); throws on unserializable values\n874\t  (`bigint` is serialized as its decimal digits, cycles throw).\n875\t\n876\t- `src/llm/retry.ts`\n877\t  ```ts\n878\t  export async function withStepRetry<R>(attempt:() => Promise<R>,\n879\t      opts:{maxRetries:number, signal:AbortSignal,\n880\t          sleep?:(ms:number, signal:AbortSignal) => Promise<void>}\n881\t  ):Promise<R>\n882\t  export function retryDelay(err:unknown, backoffMs:number):number\n883\t  ```\n884\t  Uses `err.retryable` from the adapter; aborts and `CaptureError` and\n885\t  `LlmError` cutoff errors are never retried.\n886\t\n887\t- `src/llm/review.ts` (pure)\n888\t  ```ts\n889\t  export interface ReviewInput {\n890\t      calls:readonly {id:string, name:string, input:string}[]\n891\t      finalTool:string\n892\t      toolNames:ReadonlySet<string>   // caller tools + nudge\n893\t      recordTools:ReadonlySet<string>\n894\t      finalPhase:boolean\n895\t      turn:number\n896\t      submissionAttempts:number\n897\t      recordRounds:number\n898\t      maxAttempts:number\n899\t      mayContinue:() => boolean       // 2.8, may latch the deadline\n900\t      validateFinal:(input:string) => string|null\n901\t  }\n902\t  export interface ReviewDecision {\n903\t      dispatch:boolean\n904\t      nudge?:{id:string, message:string}\n905\t      blockExploration?:string\n906\t      rejectFinal?:string\n907\t      accept?:{id:string, input:string}\n908\t      finalErr?:string|null           // undefined = unchanged\n909\t      validationExhausted:boolean\n910\t      halt:boolean\n911\t      stopCause:string\n912\t      submissionAttempts:number\n913\t      recordRounds:number\n914\t      unknownCalls:readonly {name:string, input:string}[]\n915\t  }\n916\t  export function reviewResponse(input:ReviewInput):ReviewDecision\n917\t  ```\n918\t\n919\t- `src/llm/agent.ts` (imperative shell)\n920\t  ```ts\n921\t  export interface LoopDeps {\n922\t      adapter:ProtocolAdapter\n923\t      model:string\n924\t      protocol:'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\n925\t      maxOutputTokens:number\n926\t      maxAttempts:number          // already normalized (<= 0 -> 4)\n927\t      requestTimeout:number       // ms, 0 = none\n928\t      ledger:Ledger|null\n929\t      addUsage:(u:Usage) => void  // client accumulator\n930\t      now?:() => number\n931\t      sleep?:(ms:number, signal:AbortSignal) => Promise<void>\n932\t      sessionId?:() => string     // default: 'roast-' + base32(16 bytes)\n933\t  }\n934\t  export function runConversation<T>(deps:LoopDeps, system:string,\n935\t      user:string, opts:ConverseOptions<T>, structured:boolean,\n936\t      signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n937\t  export const NUDGE_TOOL_NAME = '__roast_continue'\n938\t  export const DEFAULT_MAX_TURNS = 8\n939\t  export const DEFAULT_INPUT_BYTE_CEILING = 3_000_000\n940\t  export const DEFAULT_WRAP_UP_WINDOW = 90_000\n941\t  export const DEFAULT_MAX_ATTEMPTS = 4\n942\t  ```\n943\t  Per turn: prepare (2.5), then `withStepRetry` around one model call:\n944\t  build request, reserve, stream into a buffer with\n945\t  `deadline.callSignal` (plus request timeout), release in `finally`,\n946\t  verify capture and signal, then review and dispatch. Budget refusal\n947\t  and in-flight deadline perform the in-step switch (2.9) inside the\n948\t  same attempt. Then stop conditions (2.10).\n949\t\n950\t- `src/llm/client.ts` (shared with the protocols area): builds a\n951\t  `Client` from a resolved role, owns the usage accumulator, implements\n952\t  `converse` / `completeStructured` by calling `runConversation`.\n953\t\n954\t### 3.5 Dependencies\n955\t\n956\t- LLM protocols area: adapters, request byte size, capture, retryable\n957\t  classification, session header.\n958\t- Config / CLI areas: profile values (`model`, `max_output_tokens`,\n959\t  `protocol`) and run limits (`limits.max_attempts`,\n960\t  `limits.request_timeout`, `limits.max_total_tokens`, which sizes the\n961\t  ledger).\n962\t- Review (finder) and verify areas: callers; they build tools, the final\n963\t  spec (zod-derived JSON Schema plus decoder), deadlines, and react to\n964\t  sentinels. The verify stage reads `ledger.exhausted()` before launching\n965\t  each verification.\n966\t- CLI / records area: constructs one ledger per run and writes\n967\t  `ledger.committed()` and client usage into the run record.\n968\t- Debug: `@substrate-system/debug` namespace `roast:llm`.\n969\t\n970\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n971\t\n972\tOnly these items from `00-decisions.md` touch this area:\n973\t\n974\t- **Own loop, no framework** (Architecture, LLM): the Go loop delegated\n975\t  turn sequencing, step retries, required-field validation, unknown-tool\n976\t  results, and sequential dispatch to `charm.land/fantasy` v0.48.0. The\n977\t  TS loop implements those behaviors itself, exactly as specified in\n978\t  section 2; none of them changes. Fantasy's JSON repair of malformed\n979\t  tool arguments was disabled in Go and is absent in TS.\n980\t- **AbortSignal instead of context** (Architecture): Go contexts become\n981\t  `AbortSignal`s. Caller cancellation surfaces as `signal.reason` where\n982\t  Go returned `context.Canceled` / `context.DeadlineExceeded`. A tool\n983\t  cannot panic; any throw or rejection becomes the `{\"error\": ...}`\n984\t  result (Go: `tool panicked: <value>`).\n985\t- **Debug tracing** (Deliberate change 5): namespace `roast:llm`. Trace\n986\t  turn numbers, phase changes, cutoffs, ledger admissions/refusals\n987\t  (numbers only), retries, and stop causes. Never trace request or\n988\t  response bodies, prompts, tool arguments or results, headers, or\n989\t  credentials.\n990\t- **Case-sensitive keys** (Deliberate change 7): final-argument decoding\n991\t  matches property names exactly, where Go matched case-insensitively.\n992\t  JSON `null` still means absent.\n993\t- **Cached tokens subtracted once** (Deliberate change 13): the ledger\n994\t  and `Usage` consume disjoint counts from the llm-protocols area.\n995\t- **Process lifetime** (Cross-cutting rules): tools kill child processes\n996\t  on abort and the CLI exits explicitly after flushing output.\n997\t- **Node `>=22.18`** (Deliberate change 3): required for the\n998\t  `JSON.parse` reviver source-text argument used to preserve large\n999\t  integers, `AbortSignal.any`, and `AbortSignal.timeout`.\n1000\t\n1001\t## 5. Test plan\n1002\t\n1003\tDefault seam: the agent loop driven by a scripted fake `ProtocolAdapter`\n1004\t(a list of canned responses or a function of the request), with an\n1005\tinjected `now` and `sleep` where timing matters. Items marked\n1006\t**[HTTP]** run the real adapters against a `node:http` fake server and\n1007\tland with the protocol they need (M3 for `chat`, M4 for the others).\n1008\tAssertions use sentinels (`hasSentinel`), `LlmError.details`, call\n1009\tcounts, requests received, and tool invocations, never exact message\n1010\ttext.\n1011\t\n1012\t### 5.1 Finalization\n1013\t\n1014\t- A valid submission ends finalization in one call however the final\n1015\t  phase was reached (turn budget, budget refusal, deadline); every\n1016\t  final-phase request uses automatic tool choice and still offers the\n1017\t  final tool (TestFinalizationUsesAutomaticToolChoice).\n1018\t- Final-phase reminders never reopen exploration: an exploration call,\n1019\t  an unknown tool, or prose on the wrap-up turn is answered with a\n1020\t  reminder, the exploration tool is never run, and the next valid\n1021\t  submission succeeds (TestFinalizationRemindsWithoutReopeningExploration).\n1022\t- A model that never submits stops after exactly `maxAttempts` final\n1023\t  responses, for both `converse` and `completeStructured`, with\n1024\t  `no-final-call` (TestFinalizationMissingSubmissionIsBounded).\n1025\t- After a budget or deadline cutoff there are no reminders: one wrap-up\n1026\t  call, error matches the cutoff sentinel and `no-final-call`\n1027\t  (TestFinalizationDoesNotRetryAfterCutoff).\n1028\t- Invalid submissions and prose share one allowance; when a schema\n1029\t  failure is followed by prose, the error is `no-final-call`, not the\n1030\t  stale schema error (TestFinalizationSharesStructuredRetryAllowance).\n1031\t- A reminder needs ledger admission: reported usage that spends the\n1032\t  budget makes the next reminder fail with `budget-exhausted` +\n1033\t  `no-final-call` after one call\n1034\t  (TestFinalizationReminderRequiresTokenAdmission).\n1035\t- Caller cancellation during a reminder surfaces as the caller's abort\n1036\t  reason (TestFinalizationReminderPreservesCallerDeadline).\n1037\t- A final call missing a required property is never accepted and is\n1038\t  bounded by `maxAttempts`\n1039\t  (TestFinalizationFrameworkValidationFailureIsBounded).\n1040\t- A required-field rejection during exploration does not consume\n1041\t  final-phase attempts; the run then succeeds on the third call\n1042\t  (TestExplorationSchemaRejectionPreservesFinalizationAllowance).\n1043\t- An invalid submission in the final phase is retried with validation\n1044\t  feedback in the next request and the corrected one is returned\n1045\t  (TestAgentFinalizationRetriesInvalidSubmission,\n1046\t  TestStructuredOutputRetryUsesFantasyLoop).\n1047\t- A provider failure after a provisional schema rejection is reported as\n1048\t  `endpoint`, not as a schema error\n1049\t  (TestStructuredOutputRetryDoesNotMaskLaterProviderFailure).\n1050\t- Persistently malformed final arguments end with the schema error\n1051\t  (`details.stopCause` = invalid arguments)\n1052\t  (TestAgentReportsMalformedFinalArguments).\n1053\t- `{\"id\":9007199254740993}` reaches the caller exactly\n1054\t  (TestFinalToolPreservesLargeIntegers).\n1055\t\n1056\t### 5.2 Record tools and the deadline\n1057\t\n1058\t- Record-only wrap-up rounds do not spend submission attempts but are\n1059\t  bounded: with `maxAttempts: 1`, two calls, two records, then\n1060\t  `no-final-call` with `details.recordRounds === 2`\n1061\t  (TestWrapUpRecordRoundsAreBounded).\n1062\t- A deadline that passes while a wrap-up record tool runs lets the\n1063\t  record finish, fires `onWorkDeadline`, opens no further call, and\n1064\t  ends with `work-deadline` + `no-final-call`\n1065\t  (TestDeadlineDuringWrapUpDispatchStopsFinalization).\n1066\t- After the deadline, record tools still run (alone or next to a blocked\n1067\t  exploration call) and no second call is made\n1068\t  (TestRecordToolRunsAfterDeadlineCutoff).\n1069\t- An exploration call cut by the work deadline is replaced, in the same\n1070\t  turn, by a wrap-up call under a fresh window; `onWorkDeadline` fires;\n1071\t  the wrap-up submission succeeds\n1072\t  (TestAgentInFlightDeadlineGetsFreshFinalWindow).\n1073\t- A failed wrap-up call after the deadline is not retried, even with\n1074\t  `maxAttempts: 3` (TestAgentDoesNotRetryFailedDeadlineWrapUp).\n1075\t- A tool call buffered before an abort is never dispatched: with a\n1076\t  request timeout the run fails `endpoint` after one call; with a work\n1077\t  deadline the wrap-up call keeps the full tool set, gets a live signal,\n1078\t  and its submission succeeds\n1079\t  (TestStreamCancellationDoesNotDispatchBufferedTool).\n1080\t- A request timeout is an `endpoint` error whose cause is a timeout, and\n1081\t  it returns promptly (TestInferenceRequestTimeoutIsEndpointFailure).\n1082\t\n1083\t### 5.3 Dispatch\n1084\t\n1085\t- Prose during exploration gets a nudge result and the conversation\n1086\t  continues (TestAgentNudgesFreeFormResponse).\n1087\t- An unknown tool gets a `tool not found` error result, is reported once\n1088\t  to `onTurn`, and the run recovers (TestAgentUnknownToolReturnsErrorToModel).\n1089\t- Several tool calls in one response run sequentially in call order\n1090\t  (TestAgentExecutesMultipleToolsSequentially).\n1091\t- A final call suppresses exploration calls in the same response\n1092\t  (TestStreamFinalSubmissionSuppressesOtherToolCalls).\n1093\t- Finish reasons `length`, `content-filter`, `error`, `unknown` never\n1094\t  dispatch any tool, including a final call\n1095\t  (TestStreamAbnormalFinishNeverDispatchesTools).\n1096\t- The complete caller schema (`additionalProperties: false`) reaches the\n1097\t  adapter (TestAgentRestoresCompleteToolSchemaAtModelBoundary).\n1098\t\n1099\t### 5.4 Retries\n1100\t\n1101\t- A transient failure is retried once with `maxAttempts: 2`, honoring\n1102\t  `retry-after-ms` (TestFantasyRetriesTransientModelFailure).\n1103\t- [HTTP] An interrupted Anthropic stream is retried and its partial tool\n1104\t  call is never executed\n1105\t  (TestAnthropicInterruptedStreamRetriesWithoutExecutingPartialTools).\n1106\t\n1107\t### 5.5 Ledger and usage\n1108\t\n1109\t- Budget refusal of the first exploration call goes straight to one\n1110\t  wrap-up call that succeeds; `onBudgetExhausted` fires\n1111\t  (TestAgentBudgetRefusalUsesReservedFinalCall, ledger limit 1500,\n1112\t  `maxOutputTokens` 100).\n1113\t- Chat usage normalization: prompt tokens 8 with 4 cached, output 3 ->\n1114\t  uncached 4, cached 4, write 0, output 3, calls 1; counts are disjoint\n1115\t  and cached is subtracted once (deliberate change 13; Go test\n1116\t  TestGenericUsageAccounting, expected values updated).\n1117\t- Aborting a call mid-stream aborts the adapter's signal and releases\n1118\t  the reservation (`outstanding` back to 0)\n1119\t  (TestStreamConsumerStopCancelsAndReleasesReservation).\n1120\t- Ledger unit tests (no Go test; the ledger is a named lower seam):\n1121\t  `newLedger(0)` and negative limits give no ledger and unlimited\n1122\t  admission; final allowance is `min(100000, limit/2)`; a non-final\n1123\t  reservation is refused exactly when\n1124\t  `committed + outstanding + est > limit - max(allowance, est + out)`;\n1125\t  a final reservation may use the whole limit; any refusal latches\n1126\t  `exhausted()`; release is idempotent and reconciles with cache reads\n1127\t  at 0.1 weight and negative counts floored; `committed()` truncates.\n1128\t\n1129\t### 5.6 Error naming and provider integration\n1130\t\n1131\t- [HTTP] A truncated response names finish reason `length` and the\n1132\t  `max_output_tokens` hint; prose names the model and the submission\n1133\t  attempt count; a provider rejection during wrap-up keeps the\n1134\t  provider's error after a single generic sentinel\n1135\t  (TestNoSubmissionNamesTheCause). Port as assertions on `details`\n1136\t  (finish reason, attempts, model) plus the sentinel set.\n1137\t- [HTTP] A reasoning-only `length` / `max_tokens` finish is a\n1138\t  no-submission error (not `endpoint`), stated once, not retried\n1139\t  (TestReasoningOnlyLengthFinishNamesTheCeiling).\n1140\t- [HTTP] Abnormal provider reasons map to categories and keep the raw\n1141\t  reason (TestAbnormalFinishNamesTheProviderReason).\n1142\t- [HTTP] Thinking-mode providers get automatic tool choice\n1143\t  (TestDeepSeekThinkingModeUsesAutomaticToolChoice,\n1144\t  TestDeepSeekFixtureRejectsForcedToolChoice).\n1145\t- [HTTP] One session ID per conversation, shared by all its requests\n1146\t  and corrections, distinct across conversations\n1147\t  (TestProviderConversationIdentity,\n1148\t  TestStructuredCorrectionsRetainConversationIdentity).\n1149\t- [HTTP] Anthropic requests carry `max_tokens` = `maxOutputTokens`\n1150\t  (TestAnthropicNativeMessagesPreservesOutputBudget).\n1151\t- [HTTP] Follow-up Anthropic requests replay the signed-thinking prefix\n1152\t  (system, tools, earlier content, injected reminders) unchanged\n1153\t  (TestAnthropicReplayKeepsSignedThinkingPrefix,\n1154\t  TestBindingServerRejectsChangedPrefix).\n1155\t\n1156\t## 6. Go baseline references\n1157\t\n1158\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n1159\t\n1160\t- `internal/llm/agent.go` -- run state, turn preparation, response\n1161\t  review, final/exploration/nudge tools, error precedence.\n1162\t- `internal/llm/llm.go` -- `Usage`, `Tool`, `TurnInfo`, `ConverseOpts`,\n1163\t  `workDeadlineState`, notice text, `Client`.\n1164\t- `internal/llm/ledger.go` -- ledger constants and math.\n1165\t- `internal/llm/errors.go` -- sentinels.\n1166\t- `internal/llm/provider.go` -- `defaultMaxAttempts`,\n1167\t  `defaultInputByteCeiling`, `addUsage`, `maxAttempts`,\n1168\t  `normalizeProviderError`.\n1169\t- `charm.land/fantasy@v0.48.0` `agent.go` (step loop, validation,\n1170\t  sequential dispatch, continuation), `retry.go` and `errors.go` (retry\n1171\t  policy and retryable classification).\n1172\t- Callers: `internal/review/review.go` (finder, `report_finding` record\n1173\t  tool), `internal/verify/verify.go`, `internal/cli/doctor.go`,\n1174\t  `internal/cli/cli.go` (ledger construction, `Committed`).\n1175\t- Tests: `internal/llm/agent_test.go`, `finalization_test.go`,\n1176\t  `stream_test.go`, `session_test.go`, `anthropic_budget_test.go`,\n1177\t  `deepseek_test.go`, `stream_capture_test.go`,\n1178\t  `thinking_binding_test.go`.\n1179\t\n1180\t## 7. Resolved\n1181\t\n1182\t1. A type-invalid final call during exploration ends the run with no\n1183\t   reminder: Go parity, kept (spec.md \"Resolved open questions\").\n1184\t2. Ledger `reqBytes` = the adapter's exact request body length + 2048;\n1185\t   accepted, results near the limit may differ from Go.\n1186\t3. Tool results use sorted keys and no HTML escaping, via the llm area's\n1187\t   encoder (spec.md cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\").\n1188\t4. Decoders match JSON keys exactly (deliberate change 7); `null` means\n1189\t   absent (Go parity).\n1190\t5. Any adapter may supply a raw finish reason.\n1191\t6. Tools kill child processes on abort and the CLI calls `process.exit`\n1192\t   after flushing output (spec.md cross-cutting \"Process lifetime\").\n1193\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79353e88-0eda-49f3-aae4-5b092ab34fc4","uuid":"d9c073e2-26b6-479f-85c6-f347bef895cf","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:09:51.233Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-14-agent-loop-token-ledger/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/07-agent-loop.md","content":"# Area 07: Agent loop, token ledger, deadlines and wrap-up\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nEvery model interaction in a review goes through one conversation engine:\nthe finder's exploration conversation, each verifier conversation, the\n`doctor` probe, and every single-shot structured completion. This area\nspecifies that engine, independent of any wire protocol:\n\n- the **agent loop**: a multi-turn tool conversation that ends when the\n  model calls the role's **final tool** (for example `submit_findings`)\n  with arguments that validate, and that returns those arguments as the\n  conversation's result;\n- the **structured completion**: the same loop with only a final tool and\n  a one-turn budget;\n- the **wrap-up turn**: the reserved last call in which exploration is\n  over and the model is told to submit what it has, reached by turn\n  exhaustion, the run's **work deadline**, or the **token ledger**;\n- the **token ledger**: one per-run admission controller shared by the\n  finder and verifier clients that reserves each call's worst-case token\n  footprint before it is sent and always keeps enough headroom for the\n  wrap-up submission;\n- the error vocabulary callers use to tell a graceful cutoff (partial\n  results) from an endpoint or credential failure.\n\nGoverning invariant: **no stop path may discard accumulated work**. The\nbudget and the deadline never cancel the wrap-up call; results the model\nrecords early (record tools) always run, even after the cutoff.\n\nOut of scope here (owned by the LLM protocols area): HTTP requests, the\nthree wire protocols, SSE parsing, raw response capture into run records,\nsession headers, ambient environment handling, and classifying a provider\nerror as retryable. This file assumes the adapter interface in section 3.\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Terms\n\n- **Conversation**: one call to `converse` or `completeStructured`. It has\n  a system prompt, one initial user message, a tool list, and a final tool\n  name.\n- **Turn**: one loop iteration that sends one request to the model and\n  processes one complete response. `turn` is 1-based. A **step retry**\n  (2.11) and an **in-step switch to wrap-up** (2.8) resend within the same\n  turn; they do not increment `turn`.\n- **Final tool**: the tool whose arguments end the conversation. It has\n  no `run` function; its arguments are validated and returned.\n- **Record tool**: a tool with `recordsResult: true`. It saves part of\n  the result (the finder's `report_finding`) instead of exploring. It\n  runs on the wrap-up turn and after the work deadline.\n- **Exploration tool**: any other tool with a `run` function.\n- **Nudge tool**: the synthetic tool `__roast_continue` that Roast always\n  offers and that Roast itself \"calls\" to keep the loop going after a\n  free-form response (2.6).\n- **Final phase** (`finalTurn` flag): set once the loop is in wrap-up.\n  It never resets within a conversation.\n- **Cutoff**: the reason the final phase was forced by an external limit:\n  `work-deadline` or `budget-exhausted`. At most one cutoff is recorded;\n  the first one wins. Reaching `maxTurns` enters the final phase without\n  a cutoff.\n- **Submission attempt**: a final-phase response that is not record-only\n  (2.7). Counted per complete response, including responses later\n  rejected by required-field validation.\n- `maxAttempts`: the client's `maxAttempts` setting if > 0, else **4**.\n  It bounds three things: step retries (`maxAttempts - 1` retries per\n  request), final-phase submission attempts, and record-only rounds.\n\n### 2.2 Inputs\n\n`ConverseOptions` (all optional unless stated):\n\n| field | meaning | default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `tools` | exploration, record, and final tools, in order (required) | |\n| `finalTool` | name of the final tool; must be in `tools` (required) | |\n| `maxTurns` | turn at which the final phase starts | `<= 0` -> 8 |\n| `onTurn` | progress callback (2.10) | none |\n| `inputByteCeiling` | bytes that trigger the input warning | `<= 0` -> 3e6 |\n| `workDeadline` | absolute time (ms epoch) | none |\n| `wrapUpWindow` | ms bound for wrap-up call, record tools | `<= 0` -> 90000 |\n| `onWorkDeadline` | called once when the work deadline latches | none |\n| `onBudgetExhausted` | called once when the ledger forces wrap-up | none |\n\nClient settings used by the loop (resolved by the config and CLI\nareas): from the role's inference profile, `model` (request model name,\nalso used in error messages), `maxOutputTokens` (`max_output_tokens`;\nsent on every request and used by the ledger), and `protocol`; from the\nrun's `limits` (flag, then config, then default), `maxAttempts`\n(`max_attempts`) and `requestTimeout` (`request_timeout`, ms per\nrequest, 0 = none); and the run's shared `ledger` sized by\n`--max-tokens-total` / `limits.max_total_tokens` (CLI default\n3,000,000; an explicit 0 means no ledger). The CLI defaults\n`max_attempts` to 4 and `request_timeout` to 10 minutes; the loop's own\nfallback for `maxAttempts <= 0` is also 4.\n\nIf `finalTool` names no tool in `tools`, the call fails immediately,\nbefore any request, with the plain error\n`final tool \"<name>\" is not configured`.\n\n### 2.3 Tool set sent to the model\n\n- Every request in a conversation offers the **same** tool set: the\n  caller's tools in caller order, then the nudge tool last. The set\n  never narrows in the final phase (signed reasoning on Anthropic is\n  bound to the tool set; see 2.12). The final phase is enforced through\n  tool results instead.\n- Each caller tool is sent with its **complete** JSON Schema exactly as\n  supplied (including top-level keywords such as\n  `additionalProperties: false`), deep-copied per request so an adapter\n  cannot mutate the caller's copy. `required` lists stay arrays of\n  strings.\n- Nudge tool definition:\n  - name `__roast_continue`\n  - description `Continue the Roast tool loop after a free-form response.`\n  - schema `{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"message\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\n    \"required\":[\"message\"]}`\n- Tool choice is always **automatic**. The loop never forces a tool\n  choice: thinking-mode providers (DeepSeek, Claude with thinking) reject\n  a forced choice. Finalization relies on the notice, validation, and\n  bounded reminders.\n- `completeStructured(system, user, schemaName, finalSpec)` is a\n  conversation with exactly one tool: name `schemaName`, description\n  `Return the requested structured result.`, schema = the result's JSON\n  Schema (no `$schema`, no `$ref`/definitions, objects closed with\n  `additionalProperties: false`), plus the nudge tool. It runs with\n  `maxTurns: 1`, no deadline, and starts in the final phase (2.5).\n\n### 2.4 Transcript\n\nThe transcript is append-only. Order of a request's messages: system\nprompt, initial user message, then for each turn the assistant message,\nits tool-results message, and any user messages Roast injected, each at\nthe position where it was first sent.\n\n- The assistant message records exactly what the model produced, in\n  arrival order: text, reasoning blocks (with any signature or opaque\n  provider payload), and tool calls. Roast never edits, drops, or\n  reorders model output. Synthetic nudge calls (2.6) are appended\n  **after** the model's content.\n- The tool-results message has one result per dispatched call, in the\n  order the model called the tools (not completion order). A result is\n  either a success text or an error text (`isError: true`).\n- An injected user message (input warning, wrap-up notice) is appended\n  once, when first needed, and keeps its position for the rest of the\n  conversation; every later request, retry, and reminder resends it.\n\n### 2.5 Turn preparation (before each turn's request)\n\nIn this order:\n\n1. `turn += 1`.\n2. **Input warning**: if `turn > 1`, the warning was not yet sent, and the\n   transcript's byte size (2.13) exceeds `inputByteCeiling`, append once\n   the user message\n   `INPUT BUDGET EXHAUSTED. Do not request more files. Call \\`<final>\\`\n   now with what you have \\u2014 partial results beat none.`\n   (one line; `\\u2014` is an em dash in the actual string). This does\n   **not** enter the final phase.\n3. **Work deadline**: if the deadline check (2.8) reports fired, force the\n   final phase with cutoff `work-deadline`.\n4. **Turn budget**: if `turn >= normalizedMaxTurns`, force the final\n   phase (no cutoff). So `maxTurns: 4` gives three exploration turns and\n   makes turn 4 the wrap-up turn; `maxTurns: 1` starts in wrap-up.\n5. **Wrap-up notice**: if in the final phase and no notice was sent yet,\n   append the notice (2.9) as a user message and mark it sent. For a\n   structured completion the notice is always\n   ``Return the requested structured result via the `<final>` tool call.\n   Do not answer in prose.``\n\n### 2.6 Processing one complete response\n\nA response is complete when the adapter delivered a finish event. Before\nanything is dispatched:\n\n1. Raw response capture (protocols area) must have succeeded; a capture\n   failure fails the turn with a non-retryable error (2.11).\n2. If the turn's signal was aborted (caller cancel, request timeout, work\n   deadline), the turn fails with that abort; buffered tool calls are\n   **never** dispatched.\n3. No finish event (stream ended early) -> incomplete-stream error, which\n   is retryable (2.11). Partial tool calls are never dispatched.\n\nThen, by finish reason category (`stop`, `length`, `content-filter`,\n`tool-calls`, `error`, `other`, `unknown`):\n\n- **Abnormal finish** (anything but `stop` / `tool-calls`): nothing is\n  dispatched, not even a final call; the conversation ends. Set\n  `stopCause` = `the response ended with finish reason \"<category>\"`,\n  then append ` (the provider sent \"<raw>\")` when the adapter supplied a\n  raw provider reason that differs from the category, then, for `length`\n  only, append `; raise max_output_tokens if the output was cut off`.\n  A length finish is not retried.\n- **`stop` or `tool-calls`**: run **review** below. Its outcome says which\n  calls to dispatch (if any), which synthetic nudge calls to append, and\n  whether the conversation continues.\n\n**Review** (`stopCause` is cleared first). Let `calls` be the model's\ntool calls in order, `final` the **last** call naming the final tool,\n`records` the number of record-tool calls. Any call naming a tool that is\nneither a caller tool nor the nudge tool is reported to `onTurn`\nimmediately (2.10).\n\nA. Final phase, no final call, `records > 0`, and every call is a record\n   call (**record-only round**): increment `recordRounds`. Submission\n   attempts are **not** incremented. If finalization may not continue\n   (2.8: a cutoff exists, after latching the deadline) -> set `halt` with\n   stopCause ``the model recorded results but did not call `<final>`\n   before the cutoff``. Else if `recordRounds > maxAttempts` -> set `halt`\n   with stopCause ``the model kept recording results without calling\n   `<final>` (<n> recording rounds)``. Dispatch all calls.\n\nB. Otherwise, if in the final phase, `submissionAttempts += 1`.\n\nC. A final call is present:\n   - Every exploration call in this response gets the error result\n     ``Not executed: this response also called `<final>`, which ends the\n     run.`` and is not run. Record calls still run.\n   - **Type validation** of the final arguments (2.7). If it fails, the\n     loop is in the final phase, and another submission may be made\n     (`canRetrySubmission`: finalization may continue and\n     `submissionAttempts < maxAttempts`): this is a provisional\n     rejection. Clear any earlier `finalErr`. Every final call in the\n     response gets the error result ``your previous response was not\n     valid JSON matching the schema: <err>. Call `<final>` again with\n     valid arguments.`` Set stopCause\n     ``the last `<final>` call did not match the schema``.\n     Dispatch and continue.\n   - Otherwise set `finalErr` to the validation error (or clear it on\n     success). A failed validation here is terminal\n     (`validationExhausted`): the final call gets the error result\n     `<err>` and the conversation stops. This includes an invalid final\n     call during exploration: it ends the run immediately, without a\n     reminder.\n   - On successful type validation, the final call becomes the accepted\n     submission. Set stopCause ``the last `<final>` call did not pass\n     tool-call validation`` (used only if required-field validation, 2.7,\n     rejects it). Dispatch in order:\n     - another final call that is not the accepted one (different call ID\n       or different arguments text) gets the error result\n       ``Not accepted: call `<final>` once per response.``;\n     - the accepted call, if it passes required-field validation,\n       completes the conversation: the result is the parsed arguments,\n       its tool result text is the raw arguments string, and the loop\n       stops after this response's dispatch;\n     - a final call dispatched after the conversation already completed\n       (for example a duplicate of the accepted call) gets\n       `Already submitted.` and stops the turn.\n\nD. Final phase, no final call (prose, exploration calls, or unknown\n   tools, possibly mixed with record calls):\n   - Exploration calls get the error result\n     `Not executed: exploration is finished.`; record calls still run.\n   - If `canRetrySubmission`: append a nudge call with ID\n     `roast-final-<submissionAttempts>` and message\n     ``Exploration is finished. Return the result via the `<final>` tool\n     call, not prose or another tool.``; dispatch and continue.\n   - Else set stopCause ``the model answered without calling\n     `<final>` `` (no calls) or ``the model called other tools instead of\n     `<final>` `` (some calls), then append\n     ` (<n> submission attempt)` / ` (<n> submission attempts)` (singular\n     exactly when n = 1). If `records > 0`, set `halt` and dispatch (the\n     record calls run; everything else is blocked as above). Otherwise\n     dispatch nothing; the conversation ends.\n\nE. Exploration phase, `calls` non-empty: dispatch all calls normally.\n\nF. Exploration phase, no calls (prose only): append a nudge call with ID\n   `roast-nudge-<turn>` and message ``Results must be delivered via the\n   `<final>` tool call. If you have enough context call it now; otherwise\n   call an exploration tool.``; dispatch and continue.\n\nThe nudge tool's result is its `message` argument as success text. A\nnudge counts as a dispatched call, so the turn continues.\n\n### 2.7 Validating and dispatching tool calls\n\nDispatch is **sequential**, in the model's call order: one tool finishes\n(or is abandoned, 2.8) before the next starts. Before a call is run it\npasses **required-field validation** (applies to every tool, including\nthe final tool and the nudge tool):\n\n- unknown tool -> error result\n  `tool not found: <name>. Available tools: <n1>, <n2>, ...` (the caller\n  tools then the nudge tool, comma-space separated);\n- arguments are not a JSON object -> error result\n  `invalid JSON input: <parse error>`;\n- a top-level `required` property of the tool's schema is absent ->\n  error result `missing required parameter: <name>` (first missing name\n  in `required` order).\n\nNo repair of malformed arguments is ever attempted (Go disabled\nFantasy's JSON repair). A call that fails this validation is not run.\nFor the final tool this means no completion; the conversation continues\nif the response continues (2.10), and the stop conditions bound it. This\nvalidation does not consume anything beyond the submission attempt\nalready counted in review B.\n\n**Type validation** of final arguments (review C) mirrors decoding the\nJSON into the caller's typed result:\n\n- the text must be valid JSON (else the error is `invalid JSON`);\n- every property that is present must have a compatible type (a string\n  where a string is expected, an integer where an integer is expected,\n  and so on, recursively);\n- property names match exactly (case-sensitive; deliberate change 7), so\n  `Name` never satisfies `name`; a JSON `null` for any property counts as\n  absent (Go parity), not as a type error;\n- absent properties are allowed here (required-field validation catches\n  top-level ones later); unknown properties are ignored;\n- **integers are preserved exactly**: an integer beyond\n  `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (for example `9007199254740993`) must reach\n  the caller unchanged, never rounded. Parse the arguments with a reviver\n  that reads the literal source text and yields a `bigint` for unsafe\n  integers; the result schema accepts `bigint` for 64-bit integer fields.\n\nRunning an exploration or record call:\n\n- A blocked call (review C/D) returns its block message as an error\n  result and is not reported to `onTurn`.\n- A tool with no `run` gets the error result `{\"error\":\"tool has no\n  implementation\"}` (as success text, see below).\n- `run(args, signal)` receives the decoded argument object. If it throws\n  or rejects, or its wait is cut short (2.8), the result value is\n  `{\"error\": \"<message>\"}`.\n- The result value is serialized as JSON and sent as **success** text\n  (tool errors are data for the model, not error results). If\n  serialization fails, send `{\"error\": \"<serialization error>\"}`. An\n  `undefined` / `null` result serializes as `null`.\n- Serialization uses the llm area's one JSON encoder (spec.md\n  cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\"): object keys are sorted, matching Go's\n  map ordering so prompts stay stable, and there is no HTML escaping of\n  `<`, `>`, `&`, U+2028 or U+2029. Parity with Go is on parsed JSON, not\n  bytes.\n\n### 2.8 Work deadline and wrap-up window\n\nThe work deadline is a **latched clock check**, never a timeout around\nthe whole conversation (that would cancel the wrap-up turn along with the\nwork).\n\n- `check()`: if already fired, return true. If there is no deadline or\n  `now < deadline`, return false. Otherwise latch `fired = true`, call\n  `onWorkDeadline` exactly once, and return true.\n- Request signal for a model call: the caller's signal, combined with\n  - before the latch, a timer that fires at the deadline (so an in-flight\n    exploration call cannot run past it);\n  - after the latch, a fresh `wrapUpWindow` timeout (so a stuck wrap-up\n    cannot hang the run);\n  - and, if `requestTimeout > 0`, a per-request timeout.\n- **In-flight deadline**: if an exploration call (not final phase) fails\n  for any reason, `check()` is true afterwards, and the caller's signal is\n  not aborted (and the failure was not a capture failure), the loop forces\n  the final phase with cutoff `work-deadline` and immediately sends the\n  wrap-up call **within the same turn** (in-step switch, 2.9). The\n  failed call is not retried.\n- After every model call, `check()` runs (latching a deadline that passed\n  during the call).\n- **Exploration tool wait**: before running an exploration call, if\n  `check()` is true, the call is not started and its result is\n  `{\"error\":\"llm: work deadline reached\"}` (later calls of a multi-tool\n  response short-circuit this way). Otherwise the tool runs with a signal\n  bounded by the deadline (or unbounded with no deadline). The loop races\n  the tool's promise against that signal: on abort it stops waiting,\n  records the abort reason as the error, and abandons the promise (its\n  outcome is ignored). Tools that spawn a process kill it on abort\n  (spec.md \"Process lifetime\"), so an abandoned tool does not keep Node\n  alive; the CLI also calls `process.exit` after flushing output.\n  `check()` runs after either outcome.\n- **Record tool wait**: always bounded by a fresh `wrapUpWindow` timeout\n  from the caller's signal, before or after the deadline, so work\n  reported at the cutoff is kept rather than interrupted.\n- `mayContinueFinalization()`: run `check()`; if fired, force the final\n  phase with cutoff `work-deadline`; return `cutoff === none`. After any\n  cutoff the loop never sends a reminder and never opens a second wrap-up\n  window: the wrap-up turn gets exactly one response.\n- `canRetrySubmission()` = `mayContinueFinalization() &&\n  submissionAttempts < maxAttempts`.\n\n### 2.9 Forced wrap-up: notice text and in-step switch\n\nNotice text, chosen when it is first sent (`<final>` is the final tool):\n\n- cutoff `budget-exhausted`:\n  ``TOKEN BUDGET EXHAUSTED \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with everything you\n  have; partial results beat none.``\n- cutoff `work-deadline` and the deadline latched:\n  ``WORK DEADLINE REACHED \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with everything you\n  have; partial results beat none.``\n- otherwise (turn budget, or a `work-deadline` cutoff whose latch did not\n  happen): ``Exploration budget exhausted \\u2014 call `<final>` NOW with\n  everything you have; partial results beat none.``\n- structured completion: the structured notice in 2.5 replaces all of the\n  above.\n\nEach is one line in the actual string; `\\u2014` is an em dash.\n\n**In-step switch** (budget refusal of an exploration call, 2.13, or an\nin-flight deadline, 2.8): set the final phase, append the notice if none\nwas sent yet, rebuild the request from the transcript (same tool set),\nand send it as a final-phase call in the same turn. That response is\nprocessed like any final-phase response (review B-D). The refused or\nfailed exploration request leaves no trace in the transcript.\n\n### 2.10 Stop conditions, callbacks, and continuation\n\nAfter each turn's dispatch, evaluate in order, short-circuiting:\n\n1. the conversation completed (accepted final call);\n2. `validationExhausted`;\n3. `halt`;\n4. final phase and `submissionAttempts >= maxAttempts`;\n5. **deadline during finalization**: final phase and `check()` is true ->\n   force cutoff `work-deadline` (if no cutoff yet) and, if `stopCause` is\n   empty, set it to ``the work deadline passed before `<final>` was\n   called``.\n\nStop if any is true. Otherwise continue only if this turn dispatched at\nleast one call (including nudges) and no result requested a stop (an\naccepted final call or `Already submitted.`). A response that dispatches\nnothing ends the conversation. After dispatch, if the caller's signal is\naborted, stop with the abort (2.14) instead of continuing.\n\n`maxTurns` does not stop the loop by itself. The final phase is bounded\nby submission attempts, record rounds, cutoffs, and the deadline.\n\nCallbacks:\n\n- `onTurn({turn, tool, args})` is called after each **dispatched**\n  exploration or record call, whether it succeeded, failed, or was\n  refused by the deadline. It is also called, during review, for each\n  call to an unknown tool (nothing else would report it). It is never\n  called for the final tool, the nudge tool, blocked calls, or calls that\n  failed required-field validation. `args` is the decoded argument\n  object, or `{\"error\": \"args parse error: <message>\"}` if the arguments\n  do not parse as a JSON object. `turn` is the current turn number.\n- `onWorkDeadline()`: exactly once, at the first latch (2.8). It fires\n  even if the wrap-up submission then succeeds, so callers can mark\n  coverage as partial.\n- `onBudgetExhausted()`: at most once per conversation, when the ledger\n  refuses an exploration call, or refuses a final-phase call while no\n  cutoff is recorded yet. Fires even if the wrap-up then succeeds.\n\n### 2.11 Step retries\n\nEach turn's request (and each in-step wrap-up request) is retried when it\nfails with a retryable error, up to `maxAttempts - 1` retries:\n\n- Retryable (classified by the adapter): HTTP 408, 409, 429, any 5xx; an\n  error flagged transient; a response header `x-should-retry` (any case)\n  parsing as true; an unexpectedly ended stream (no finish event);\n  network/transport errors (DNS, connection refused/reset, TCP timeout,\n  HTTP/2 stream errors).\n- Never retried: aborts (caller cancel, request timeout, deadline\n  signal), capture failures, ledger refusals, any error after a cutoff\n  in the final phase (2.14 wraps it into a cutoff error), and every\n  other error.\n- Delay: initial 5,000 ms, doubled after each retry. A `retry-after-ms`\n  header (milliseconds, float) or else `retry-after` (seconds, float, or\n  an HTTP date) replaces the delay when the parsed value is > 0 and\n  (< 60 s or < the backoff delay). The wait aborts with the caller's\n  signal.\n- Each retry re-runs ledger admission (the previous reservation was\n  released with zero usage) and resends the same transcript; any\n  provisional state from the failed attempt is discarded.\n- When retries are exhausted the error reports the last attempt's error\n  (Go prefixed `retry error: `).\n- `maxAttempts: 1` means no retries.\n\n### 2.12 Signed reasoning and conversation identity\n\n- Signed reasoning (Claude 5.1 and later) is bound to the system prompt,\n  the tool set, and every earlier message. Hence: the tool set is\n  constant (2.3), model output is replayed verbatim (2.4), corrections\n  travel as tool results rather than edits, and injected messages never\n  move.\n- Each conversation gets a session ID `roast-<26 random characters from\n  A-Z2-7>` (128 bits, base32), generated once per `converse` /\n  `completeStructured` call and passed to the adapter for every request,\n  retry, reminder, and correction of that conversation. Distinct\n  conversations get distinct IDs. (The header itself is the protocols\n  area's.)\n\n### 2.13 Token ledger\n\nOne ledger per run, shared by the finder and verifier clients (concurrent\nverifier conversations reserve against the same ledger). Units are\n\"input-equivalent tokens\". Constants:\n\n- `EST_BYTES_PER_TOKEN = 3` (conservative tokenization: code-heavy\n  prompts run about 3 bytes/token, so this over-estimates, the safe\n  direction for admission);\n- `DEFAULT_FINAL_ALLOWANCE = 100_000`;\n- `CACHED_TOKEN_WEIGHT = 0.1` (cache reads bill about 0.1x).\n\nConstruction: `newLedger(limit)` returns **no ledger** (unlimited) when\n`limit <= 0`. Otherwise `finalAllowance = min(100_000, limit / 2)`\n(floating point, not rounded). All arithmetic is floating point.\nState: `committed`, `outstanding` (both start at 0), `exhausted` (false).\n\n`reserve(reqBytes, maxOutput, final)`:\n\n```\nest = reqBytes / 3 + maxOutput\nheadroom = limit\nif not final:\n    headroom -= max(finalAllowance, est + maxOutput)\nif committed + outstanding + est > headroom:\n    exhausted = true\n    refuse\noutstanding += est\nreturn release\n```\n\nThe non-final hold-back covers this request's own footprint plus a\nworst-case response, because the wrap-up call replays the same transcript\ngrown by this call's output; a smaller hold-back could admit exploration\nthe final flush can no longer afford. A final reservation may use the\nwhole limit.\n\n`release(usage)` must be called exactly once per successful reservation;\nextra calls are no-ops. It does `outstanding -= est` and\n`committed += weighted(usage)` where\n`weighted = max(uncached,0) + 0.1 * max(cached,0) + max(cacheWrite,0)\n+ max(output,0)`. Each field is floored independently; cached may exceed\nuncached legitimately and is never clamped against it.\n\n`exhausted()` is true once any reservation was refused (final or not);\nthe verify stage reads it to stop launching verifications.\n`committed()` returns `{spent: trunc(committed), limit: trunc(limit)}`\n(`{0, 0}` with no ledger), for the run record's budget provenance.\n\nWith no ledger every reservation is admitted and release is a no-op.\n\n**Admission in the loop.** Every model request is reserved immediately\nbefore it is sent:\n\n- `reqBytes` = the adapter-reported byte size of the serialized request\n  body + 2048. (Go measured its internal transcript JSON plus the tool\n  definitions JSON plus 2048.) Admission near the limit can therefore\n  differ from Go (accepted, spec.md \"Resolved open questions\").\n- `maxOutput` = the client's `maxOutputTokens`.\n- `final` = the loop is in the final phase.\n- Release with the normalized usage (2.15) when the response delivered a\n  finish event, and with zero usage in every other outcome (error, abort,\n  capture failure, early stop). Release happens on every path, exactly\n  once.\n\nOn refusal:\n\n- exploration request: force the final phase with cutoff\n  `budget-exhausted`, fire `onBudgetExhausted`, and switch in-step to\n  the wrap-up call (2.9). The refused request is not sent.\n- final-phase request: if no cutoff is recorded yet, record\n  `budget-exhausted` and fire `onBudgetExhausted`. The turn fails with\n  the cutoff error ``...; no headroom for the wrap-up call: ...`` (2.14),\n  not retried.\n\nSo a final-phase reminder is still subject to admission: after a\nresponse whose reported usage spent the budget, the next reminder is\nrefused and the conversation ends with the budget cutoff.\n\n**Transcript bytes** (for the input warning, 2.5): the UTF-8 byte length\nof the JSON serialization of the transcript's messages, system prompt\nincluded, tool definitions excluded.\n\n### 2.14 Errors and result precedence\n\nSentinels (message texts are the Go texts):\n\n| sentinel | message |\n| --- | --- |\n| `no-final-call` | `llm: tool loop ended without a final tool call` |\n| `work-deadline` | `llm: work deadline reached` |\n| `budget-exhausted` | `llm: run token budget exhausted` |\n| `auth` | `llm: authentication failed` |\n| `endpoint` | `llm: inference endpoint failure` |\n\n`no-final-call` means finalization ran out of bounded attempts without a\nvalid submission: the finder reports incomplete coverage; the verifier\nleaves the candidate unverified (never refuted). The CLI treats\n`budget-exhausted`, `work-deadline`, and `no-final-call` as graceful.\n\n**Cutoff error** `cutoffError(what, cause?)`: matches the cutoff sentinel\n(or `no-final-call` if no cutoff) **and** `no-final-call`. Message:\n`<cutoff sentinel message>; <what>: <no-final-call message>` followed by\n`: <cause message>` when there is a cause. The cause contributes text\nonly; it is not matchable (a `budget-exhausted` cause inside a\n`work-deadline` cutoff does not make the error match\n`budget-exhausted`). The provider's own error text appears after the\ngeneric sentinel, and the `no-final-call` text appears exactly once.\n\n**Provider error normalization** `normalize(err)`:\n\n1. caller's signal aborted -> the caller's abort reason, unwrapped;\n2. err is an abort/timeout (for example `requestTimeout`) -> `endpoint`\n   error `llm: inference endpoint failure: <model>: <err>`, keeping err\n   as the cause (so a timeout stays detectable);\n3. err already matches `auth`, `endpoint`, or `budget-exhausted` -> as is;\n4. HTTP status 401 or 403 -> `auth` error\n   `llm: authentication failed: <err>; check the configured API key`;\n5. anything else -> `endpoint` error as in 2.\n\n**Final-phase request failure after a cutoff** (the request fails and a\ncutoff is recorded): the turn fails with\n`cutoffError(\"wrap-up call failed\", normalize(err))`, which is never\nretried. A capture failure is reported unwrapped instead.\n\n**Result precedence** when the loop ends:\n\n1. accepted final call -> return the parsed result.\n2. `finalErr` set -> error\n   `<model>: <final> arguments did not satisfy the schema (<err>); if\n   truncated, raise max_output_tokens`; if a cutoff is recorded, wrap it\n   as `cutoffError(\"wrap-up submission was invalid\", that)`.\n   (`finalErr` is cleared on each provisional rejection, so an earlier\n   schema failure never masks a later provider failure or a later\n   missing submission.)\n3. the loop failed with an error:\n   - already a cutoff error (matches `no-final-call`) -> as is;\n   - a cutoff is recorded -> `cutoffError(\"wrap-up turn failed\", err)`;\n   - otherwise `normalize(err)`.\n4. no error: let `cause = \"<model>: <stopCause>\"` if stopCause is set.\n   - cutoff recorded -> `cutoffError(\"wrap-up turn produced no\n     submission\", cause)`;\n   - else `no-final-call` error `<no-final-call message>: <cause>`, or\n     the bare `no-final-call` error without a cause.\n\nCaller cancellation therefore surfaces as the raw abort reason (unless a\ncutoff was already recorded, in which case it is the cause text of a\ncutoff error).\n\n### 2.15 Usage accounting\n\nAdapters report per-response counts: `input`, `cacheRead`, `cacheWrite`,\n`output`. Each is floored at 0. The client normalizes them once per\nfinished response into **disjoint** counts. The per-protocol rules are\nowned by the llm-protocols area, which subtracts cached tokens exactly\nonce for `chat` and `responses` (deliberate change 13; Go subtracted\nthem twice); `anthropic` counts are disjoint already. The loop and\nledger only consume the resulting `Usage`, where `uncached_input_tokens`,\n`cached_input_tokens`, and `cache_write_tokens` never overlap.\n\n`Usage = {uncached_input_tokens, cached_input_tokens, cache_write_tokens,\noutput_tokens, calls}` with `calls = 1` per finished response. The client\naccumulates it across all its conversations (`client.usage()`), and the\nsame per-response value reconciles the ledger. Responses without a finish\nevent add nothing. Example (chat): `prompt_tokens` 8 of which 4 cached,\n`completion_tokens` 3 -> uncached 4, cached 4, write 0, output 3,\ncalls 1. Its ledger weight is `4 + 0.1 * 4 + 0 + 3 = 7.4`.\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\nAll modules live under `src/llm/`. The loop is written as a small\nimperative shell (`agent.ts`) around pure decision functions\n(`review.ts`, `ledger.ts` math, `errors.ts`), so most invariants are unit\ntestable with a scripted fake adapter and an injected clock.\n\n### 3.1 Adapter interface (assumed; owned by the LLM protocols area)\n\n```ts\nexport type FinishCategory = 'stop'|'length'|'content-filter'|\n    'tool-calls'|'error'|'other'|'unknown'\n\nexport interface RawUsage {\n    input:number\n    cacheRead:number\n    cacheWrite:number\n    output:number\n}\n\nexport type StreamEvent =\n    | {type:'text', text:string}\n    | {type:'reasoning', part:ReasoningPart}  // opaque, replayed verbatim\n    | {type:'tool-call', id:string, name:string, input:string}\n    | {type:'finish', reason:FinishCategory, rawReason?:string,\n        usage:RawUsage}\n\nexport interface ModelRequest {\n    model:string\n    system:string\n    messages:readonly Message[]\n    tools:readonly ToolDefinition[]  // name, description, full schema\n    toolChoice:'auto'\n    maxOutputTokens:number\n    sessionId:string\n}\n\nexport interface ProtocolAdapter {\n    readonly protocol:'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\n    // Exact byte size of the serialized request body.\n    requestBytes(req:ModelRequest):number\n    // Yields events in arrival order; throws on transport/HTTP/stream\n    // errors. Capture failures throw CaptureError. Thrown errors expose\n    // `retryable:boolean` and, for HTTP failures, `status:number`.\n    stream(req:ModelRequest, signal:AbortSignal):AsyncIterable<StreamEvent>\n}\n```\n\nThe loop collects a whole response before acting on it; it does not\nforward partial text anywhere.\n\n### 3.2 Transcript (`src/llm/messages.ts`)\n\n```ts\nexport type AssistantPart =\n    | {type:'text', text:string}\n    | {type:'reasoning', part:ReasoningPart}\n    | {type:'tool-call', id:string, name:string, input:string}\n\nexport interface ToolResult {\n    callId:string\n    name:string\n    text:string\n    isError:boolean\n}\n\nexport type Message =\n    | {role:'user', text:string}\n    | {role:'assistant', parts:readonly AssistantPart[]}\n    | {role:'tool', results:readonly ToolResult[]}\n\nexport function transcriptBytes(system:string,\n    messages:readonly Message[]):number\n```\n\n### 3.3 Public types (`src/llm/types.ts`)\n\n```ts\nexport interface Tool {\n    name:string\n    description:string\n    schema:JsonSchemaObject\n    // Absent for the final tool.\n    run?:(args:Record<string, unknown>, signal:AbortSignal) =>\n        unknown|Promise<unknown>\n    recordsResult?:boolean\n}\n\nexport interface TurnInfo {\n    turn:number\n    tool:string\n    args:Record<string, unknown>\n}\n\n// Type validation of the final arguments (2.7). `decode` receives the\n// value parsed with integer preservation and throws an Error whose\n// message is the validation detail.\nexport interface FinalSpec<T> {\n    decode(value:unknown):T\n}\n\nexport interface ConverseOptions<T> {\n    tools:readonly Tool[]\n    finalTool:string\n    final:FinalSpec<T>\n    maxTurns?:number\n    onTurn?:(info:TurnInfo) => void\n    inputByteCeiling?:number\n    workDeadline?:number      // ms since epoch\n    wrapUpWindow?:number      // ms\n    onWorkDeadline?:() => void\n    onBudgetExhausted?:() => void\n}\n\nexport interface Usage {\n    uncached_input_tokens:number\n    cached_input_tokens:number\n    cache_write_tokens:number\n    output_tokens:number\n    calls:number\n}\n\nexport interface Client {\n    readonly name:string  // request model name\n    converse<T>(system:string, user:string, opts:ConverseOptions<T>,\n        signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n    completeStructured<T>(system:string, user:string, schemaName:string,\n        result:{schema:JsonSchemaObject, decode:FinalSpec<T>['decode']},\n        signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n    usage():Usage\n}\n```\n\n`Usage` keeps snake_case keys because it is serialized into run records\nas is.\n\n### 3.4 Modules\n\n- `src/llm/errors.ts`\n  ```ts\n  export type Sentinel = 'no-final-call'|'work-deadline'|\n      'budget-exhausted'|'auth'|'endpoint'\n  export interface ErrorDetails {\n      model?:string\n      finishReason?:FinishCategory\n      rawFinishReason?:string\n      submissionAttempts?:number\n      recordRounds?:number\n      stopCause?:StopCauseKind\n  }\n  export class LlmError extends Error {\n      readonly sentinels:ReadonlySet<Sentinel>\n      readonly details:ErrorDetails\n  }\n  export function hasSentinel(err:unknown, s:Sentinel):boolean\n  export function cutoffError(cutoff:Sentinel|null, what:string,\n      cause?:unknown):LlmError\n  export function normalizeProviderError(callerSignal:AbortSignal,\n      model:string, err:unknown):unknown\n  export class CaptureError extends Error {}\n  ```\n  `hasSentinel` walks `sentinels` and the `cause` chain (so an endpoint\n  error wrapping a timeout still exposes the timeout via `cause`).\n  `cutoffError` must not put the cause in the matchable chain (2.14):\n  store it as `details`-only text, not as `cause`. `details` lets tests\n  assert the stop reason, counts, and finish reason without matching\n  message text. This module is shared with the protocols area, which\n  raises `auth` / `endpoint` errors.\n\n- `src/llm/ledger.ts`\n  ```ts\n  export const EST_BYTES_PER_TOKEN = 3\n  export const DEFAULT_FINAL_ALLOWANCE = 100_000\n  export const CACHED_TOKEN_WEIGHT = 0.1\n  export type Release = (usage:Usage) => void\n  export class Ledger {\n      reserve(reqBytes:number, maxOutput:number,\n          final:boolean):Release|null   // null = refused\n      exhausted():boolean\n      committed():{spent:number, limit:number}\n  }\n  export function newLedger(limit:number):Ledger|null\n  export function reserve(ledger:Ledger|null, reqBytes:number,\n      maxOutput:number, final:boolean):Release|null\n  export function weightedTokens(usage:Usage):number\n  ```\n  All methods are synchronous, so concurrent verifier promises cannot\n  interleave inside a reservation. `Release` is idempotent.\n\n- `src/llm/usage.ts`: `normalizeUsage(protocol, raw):Usage` (2.15) and\n  `addUsage(total, u):Usage`.\n\n- `src/llm/deadline.ts`\n  ```ts\n  export class WorkDeadline {\n      constructor(opts:{deadline?:number, window?:number,\n          onFire?:() => void, now?:() => number})\n      get fired():boolean\n      check():boolean\n      // Signal for a model call: caller + deadline timer before the\n      // latch, caller + fresh window after it.\n      callSignal(caller:AbortSignal):AbortSignal\n      runTool(tool:Tool, args:Record<string, unknown>,\n          caller:AbortSignal):Promise<{value:unknown}|{error:Error}>\n  }\n  ```\n  `callSignal` and `runTool` use `AbortSignal.any` with\n  `AbortSignal.timeout` (window, request timeout) or a timer aborting at\n  the absolute deadline. `runTool` races the tool against the signal and\n  never awaits an abandoned tool.\n\n- `src/llm/final-args.ts`\n  - `parsePreservingIntegers(text:string):unknown` -- `JSON.parse` with a\n    reviver using the source-text context argument; an integer literal\n    outside the safe range becomes `bigint`. Throws on invalid JSON.\n  - `checkRequired(schema, input:string):string|null` -- the\n    required-field validation of 2.7; returns the error text or null.\n  - `typeValidate<T>(text, spec:FinalSpec<T>)` ->\n    `{ok:true, value:T}|{ok:false, error:string}` (`invalid JSON` on\n    parse failure).\n\n- `src/llm/tool-json.ts`: `goJsonStringify(value):string` -- JSON with\n  sorted keys and no HTML escaping (2.7); throws on unserializable values\n  (`bigint` is serialized as its decimal digits, cycles throw).\n\n- `src/llm/retry.ts`\n  ```ts\n  export async function withStepRetry<R>(attempt:() => Promise<R>,\n      opts:{maxRetries:number, signal:AbortSignal,\n          sleep?:(ms:number, signal:AbortSignal) => Promise<void>}\n  ):Promise<R>\n  export function retryDelay(err:unknown, backoffMs:number):number\n  ```\n  Uses `err.retryable` from the adapter; aborts and `CaptureError` and\n  `LlmError` cutoff errors are never retried.\n\n- `src/llm/review.ts` (pure)\n  ```ts\n  export interface ReviewInput {\n      calls:readonly {id:string, name:string, input:string}[]\n      finalTool:string\n      toolNames:ReadonlySet<string>   // caller tools + nudge\n      recordTools:ReadonlySet<string>\n      finalPhase:boolean\n      turn:number\n      submissionAttempts:number\n      recordRounds:number\n      maxAttempts:number\n      mayContinue:() => boolean       // 2.8, may latch the deadline\n      validateFinal:(input:string) => string|null\n  }\n  export interface ReviewDecision {\n      dispatch:boolean\n      nudge?:{id:string, message:string}\n      blockExploration?:string\n      rejectFinal?:string\n      accept?:{id:string, input:string}\n      finalErr?:string|null           // undefined = unchanged\n      validationExhausted:boolean\n      halt:boolean\n      stopCause:string\n      submissionAttempts:number\n      recordRounds:number\n      unknownCalls:readonly {name:string, input:string}[]\n  }\n  export function reviewResponse(input:ReviewInput):ReviewDecision\n  ```\n\n- `src/llm/agent.ts` (imperative shell)\n  ```ts\n  export interface LoopDeps {\n      adapter:ProtocolAdapter\n      model:string\n      protocol:'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\n      maxOutputTokens:number\n      maxAttempts:number          // already normalized (<= 0 -> 4)\n      requestTimeout:number       // ms, 0 = none\n      ledger:Ledger|null\n      addUsage:(u:Usage) => void  // client accumulator\n      now?:() => number\n      sleep?:(ms:number, signal:AbortSignal) => Promise<void>\n      sessionId?:() => string     // default: 'roast-' + base32(16 bytes)\n  }\n  export function runConversation<T>(deps:LoopDeps, system:string,\n      user:string, opts:ConverseOptions<T>, structured:boolean,\n      signal:AbortSignal):Promise<T>\n  export const NUDGE_TOOL_NAME = '__roast_continue'\n  export const DEFAULT_MAX_TURNS = 8\n  export const DEFAULT_INPUT_BYTE_CEILING = 3_000_000\n  export const DEFAULT_WRAP_UP_WINDOW = 90_000\n  export const DEFAULT_MAX_ATTEMPTS = 4\n  ```\n  Per turn: prepare (2.5), then `withStepRetry` around one model call:\n  build request, reserve, stream into a buffer with\n  `deadline.callSignal` (plus request timeout), release in `finally`,\n  verify capture and signal, then review and dispatch. Budget refusal\n  and in-flight deadline perform the in-step switch (2.9) inside the\n  same attempt. Then stop conditions (2.10).\n\n- `src/llm/client.ts` (shared with the protocols area): builds a\n  `Client` from a resolved role, owns the usage accumulator, implements\n  `converse` / `completeStructured` by calling `runConversation`.\n\n### 3.5 Dependencies\n\n- LLM protocols area: adapters, request byte size, capture, retryable\n  classification, session header.\n- Config / CLI areas: profile values (`model`, `max_output_tokens`,\n  `protocol`) and run limits (`limits.max_attempts`,\n  `limits.request_timeout`, `limits.max_total_tokens`, which sizes the\n  ledger).\n- Review (finder) and verify areas: callers; they build tools, the final\n  spec (zod-derived JSON Schema plus decoder), deadlines, and react to\n  sentinels. The verify stage reads `ledger.exhausted()` before launching\n  each verification.\n- CLI / records area: constructs one ledger per run and writes\n  `ledger.committed()` and client usage into the run record.\n- Debug: `@substrate-system/debug` namespace `roast:llm`.\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\nOnly these items from `00-decisions.md` touch this area:\n\n- **Own loop, no framework** (Architecture, LLM): the Go loop delegated\n  turn sequencing, step retries, required-field validation, unknown-tool\n  results, and sequential dispatch to `charm.land/fantasy` v0.48.0. The\n  TS loop implements those behaviors itself, exactly as specified in\n  section 2; none of them changes. Fantasy's JSON repair of malformed\n  tool arguments was disabled in Go and is absent in TS.\n- **AbortSignal instead of context** (Architecture): Go contexts become\n  `AbortSignal`s. Caller cancellation surfaces as `signal.reason` where\n  Go returned `context.Canceled` / `context.DeadlineExceeded`. A tool\n  cannot panic; any throw or rejection becomes the `{\"error\": ...}`\n  result (Go: `tool panicked: <value>`).\n- **Debug tracing** (Deliberate change 5): namespace `roast:llm`. Trace\n  turn numbers, phase changes, cutoffs, ledger admissions/refusals\n  (numbers only), retries, and stop causes. Never trace request or\n  response bodies, prompts, tool arguments or results, headers, or\n  credentials.\n- **Case-sensitive keys** (Deliberate change 7): final-argument decoding\n  matches property names exactly, where Go matched case-insensitively.\n  JSON `null` still means absent.\n- **Cached tokens subtracted once** (Deliberate change 13): the ledger\n  and `Usage` consume disjoint counts from the llm-protocols area.\n- **Process lifetime** (Cross-cutting rules): tools kill child processes\n  on abort and the CLI exits explicitly after flushing output.\n- **Node `>=22.18`** (Deliberate change 3): required for the\n  `JSON.parse` reviver source-text argument used to preserve large\n  integers, `AbortSignal.any`, and `AbortSignal.timeout`.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nDefault seam: the agent loop driven by a scripted fake `ProtocolAdapter`\n(a list of canned responses or a function of the request), with an\ninjected `now` and `sleep` where timing matters. Items marked\n**[HTTP]** run the real adapters against a `node:http` fake server and\nland with the protocol they need (M3 for `chat`, M4 for the others).\nAssertions use sentinels (`hasSentinel`), `LlmError.details`, call\ncounts, requests received, and tool invocations, never exact message\ntext.\n\n### 5.1 Finalization\n\n- A valid submission ends finalization in one call however the final\n  phase was reached (turn budget, budget refusal, deadline); every\n  final-phase request uses automatic tool choice and still offers the\n  final tool (TestFinalizationUsesAutomaticToolChoice).\n- Final-phase reminders never reopen exploration: an exploration call,\n  an unknown tool, or prose on the wrap-up turn is answered with a\n  reminder, the exploration tool is never run, and the next valid\n  submission succeeds (TestFinalizationRemindsWithoutReopeningExploration).\n- A model that never submits stops after exactly `maxAttempts` final\n  responses, for both `converse` and `completeStructured`, with\n  `no-final-call` (TestFinalizationMissingSubmissionIsBounded).\n- After a budget or deadline cutoff there are no reminders: one wrap-up\n  call, error matches the cutoff sentinel and `no-final-call`\n  (TestFinalizationDoesNotRetryAfterCutoff).\n- Invalid submissions and prose share one allowance; when a schema\n  failure is followed by prose, the error is `no-final-call`, not the\n  stale schema error (TestFinalizationSharesStructuredRetryAllowance).\n- A reminder needs ledger admission: reported usage that spends the\n  budget makes the next reminder fail with `budget-exhausted` +\n  `no-final-call` after one call\n  (TestFinalizationReminderRequiresTokenAdmission).\n- Caller cancellation during a reminder surfaces as the caller's abort\n  reason (TestFinalizationReminderPreservesCallerDeadline).\n- A final call missing a required property is never accepted and is\n  bounded by `maxAttempts`\n  (TestFinalizationFrameworkValidationFailureIsBounded).\n- A required-field rejection during exploration does not consume\n  final-phase attempts; the run then succeeds on the third call\n  (TestExplorationSchemaRejectionPreservesFinalizationAllowance).\n- An invalid submission in the final phase is retried with validation\n  feedback in the next request and the corrected one is returned\n  (TestAgentFinalizationRetriesInvalidSubmission,\n  TestStructuredOutputRetryUsesFantasyLoop).\n- A provider failure after a provisional schema rejection is reported as\n  `endpoint`, not as a schema error\n  (TestStructuredOutputRetryDoesNotMaskLaterProviderFailure).\n- Persistently malformed final arguments end with the schema error\n  (`details.stopCause` = invalid arguments)\n  (TestAgentReportsMalformedFinalArguments).\n- `{\"id\":9007199254740993}` reaches the caller exactly\n  (TestFinalToolPreservesLargeIntegers).\n\n### 5.2 Record tools and the deadline\n\n- Record-only wrap-up rounds do not spend submission attempts but are\n  bounded: with `maxAttempts: 1`, two calls, two records, then\n  `no-final-call` with `details.recordRounds === 2`\n  (TestWrapUpRecordRoundsAreBounded).\n- A deadline that passes while a wrap-up record tool runs lets the\n  record finish, fires `onWorkDeadline`, opens no further call, and\n  ends with `work-deadline` + `no-final-call`\n  (TestDeadlineDuringWrapUpDispatchStopsFinalization).\n- After the deadline, record tools still run (alone or next to a blocked\n  exploration call) and no second call is made\n  (TestRecordToolRunsAfterDeadlineCutoff).\n- An exploration call cut by the work deadline is replaced, in the same\n  turn, by a wrap-up call under a fresh window; `onWorkDeadline` fires;\n  the wrap-up submission succeeds\n  (TestAgentInFlightDeadlineGetsFreshFinalWindow).\n- A failed wrap-up call after the deadline is not retried, even with\n  `maxAttempts: 3` (TestAgentDoesNotRetryFailedDeadlineWrapUp).\n- A tool call buffered before an abort is never dispatched: with a\n  request timeout the run fails `endpoint` after one call; with a work\n  deadline the wrap-up call keeps the full tool set, gets a live signal,\n  and its submission succeeds\n  (TestStreamCancellationDoesNotDispatchBufferedTool).\n- A request timeout is an `endpoint` error whose cause is a timeout, and\n  it returns promptly (TestInferenceRequestTimeoutIsEndpointFailure).\n\n### 5.3 Dispatch\n\n- Prose during exploration gets a nudge result and the conversation\n  continues (TestAgentNudgesFreeFormResponse).\n- An unknown tool gets a `tool not found` error result, is reported once\n  to `onTurn`, and the run recovers (TestAgentUnknownToolReturnsErrorToModel).\n- Several tool calls in one response run sequentially in call order\n  (TestAgentExecutesMultipleToolsSequentially).\n- A final call suppresses exploration calls in the same response\n  (TestStreamFinalSubmissionSuppressesOtherToolCalls).\n- Finish reasons `length`, `content-filter`, `error`, `unknown` never\n  dispatch any tool, including a final call\n  (TestStreamAbnormalFinishNeverDispatchesTools).\n- The complete caller schema (`additionalProperties: false`) reaches the\n  adapter (TestAgentRestoresCompleteToolSchemaAtModelBoundary).\n\n### 5.4 Retries\n\n- A transient failure is retried once with `maxAttempts: 2`, honoring\n  `retry-after-ms` (TestFantasyRetriesTransientModelFailure).\n- [HTTP] An interrupted Anthropic stream is retried and its partial tool\n  call is never executed\n  (TestAnthropicInterruptedStreamRetriesWithoutExecutingPartialTools).\n\n### 5.5 Ledger and usage\n\n- Budget refusal of the first exploration call goes straight to one\n  wrap-up call that succeeds; `onBudgetExhausted` fires\n  (TestAgentBudgetRefusalUsesReservedFinalCall, ledger limit 1500,\n  `maxOutputTokens` 100).\n- Chat usage normalization: prompt tokens 8 with 4 cached, output 3 ->\n  uncached 4, cached 4, write 0, output 3, calls 1; counts are disjoint\n  and cached is subtracted once (deliberate change 13; Go test\n  TestGenericUsageAccounting, expected values updated).\n- Aborting a call mid-stream aborts the adapter's signal and releases\n  the reservation (`outstanding` back to 0)\n  (TestStreamConsumerStopCancelsAndReleasesReservation).\n- Ledger unit tests (no Go test; the ledger is a named lower seam):\n  `newLedger(0)` and negative limits give no ledger and unlimited\n  admission; final allowance is `min(100000, limit/2)`; a non-final\n  reservation is refused exactly when\n  `committed + outstanding + est > limit - max(allowance, est + out)`;\n  a final reservation may use the whole limit; any refusal latches\n  `exhausted()`; release is idempotent and reconciles with cache reads\n  at 0.1 weight and negative counts floored; `committed()` truncates.\n\n### 5.6 Error naming and provider integration\n\n- [HTTP] A truncated response names finish reason `length` and the\n  `max_output_tokens` hint; prose names the model and the submission\n  attempt count; a provider rejection during wrap-up keeps the\n  provider's error after a single generic sentinel\n  (TestNoSubmissionNamesTheCause). Port as assertions on `details`\n  (finish reason, attempts, model) plus the sentinel set.\n- [HTTP] A reasoning-only `length` / `max_tokens` finish is a\n  no-submission error (not `endpoint`), stated once, not retried\n  (TestReasoningOnlyLengthFinishNamesTheCeiling).\n- [HTTP] Abnormal provider reasons map to categories and keep the raw\n  reason (TestAbnormalFinishNamesTheProviderReason).\n- [HTTP] Thinking-mode providers get automatic tool choice\n  (TestDeepSeekThinkingModeUsesAutomaticToolChoice,\n  TestDeepSeekFixtureRejectsForcedToolChoice).\n- [HTTP] One session ID per conversation, shared by all its requests\n  and corrections, distinct across conversations\n  (TestProviderConversationIdentity,\n  TestStructuredCorrectionsRetainConversationIdentity).\n- [HTTP] Anthropic requests carry `max_tokens` = `maxOutputTokens`\n  (TestAnthropicNativeMessagesPreservesOutputBudget).\n- [HTTP] Follow-up Anthropic requests replay the signed-thinking prefix\n  (system, tools, earlier content, injected reminders) unchanged\n  (TestAnthropicReplayKeepsSignedThinkingPrefix,\n  TestBindingServerRejectsChangedPrefix).\n\n## 6. Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/llm/agent.go` -- run state, turn preparation, response\n  review, final/exploration/nudge tools, error precedence.\n- `internal/llm/llm.go` -- `Usage`, `Tool`, `TurnInfo`, `ConverseOpts`,\n  `workDeadlineState`, notice text, `Client`.\n- `internal/llm/ledger.go` -- ledger constants and math.\n- `internal/llm/errors.go` -- sentinels.\n- `internal/llm/provider.go` -- `defaultMaxAttempts`,\n  `defaultInputByteCeiling`, `addUsage`, `maxAttempts`,\n  `normalizeProviderError`.\n- `charm.land/fantasy@v0.48.0` `agent.go` (step loop, validation,\n  sequential dispatch, continuation), `retry.go` and `errors.go` (retry\n  policy and retryable classification).\n- Callers: `internal/review/review.go` (finder, `report_finding` record\n  tool), `internal/verify/verify.go`, `internal/cli/doctor.go`,\n  `internal/cli/cli.go` (ledger construction, `Committed`).\n- Tests: `internal/llm/agent_test.go`, `finalization_test.go`,\n  `stream_test.go`, `session_test.go`, `anthropic_budget_test.go`,\n  `deepseek_test.go`, `stream_capture_test.go`,\n  `thinking_binding_test.go`.\n\n## 7. Resolved\n\n1. A type-invalid final call during exploration ends the run with no\n   reminder: Go parity, kept (spec.md \"Resolved open questions\").\n2. Ledger `reqBytes` = the adapter's exact request body length + 2048;\n   accepted, results near the limit may differ from Go.\n3. Tool results use sorted keys and no HTML escaping, via the llm area's\n   encoder (spec.md cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\").\n4. Decoders match JSON keys exactly (deliberate change 7); `null` means\n   absent (Go parity).\n5. Any adapter may supply a raw finish reason.\n6. Tools kill child processes on abort and the CLI calls `process.exit`\n   after flushing output (spec.md cross-cutting \"Process lifetime\").\n","numLines":1193,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1193}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DGMYKU9oNBdHUwKnKDh8HM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
